{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t## §7. The Universal Continuation Format (Proposed)\n     2\t\n     3\t> [!WARNING]  \n     4\t> **Status: Proposed / Deferred.** The Semantic VM's linear CESK state is theoretically sound as an architecture-agnostic continuation format, but true heterogeneous network migration requires addressing several critical defects identified in the 2026-09-14 architectural review.\n     5\t\n     6\tThe Semantic VM's linear CESK state (`{:segment id, :pc n, :env E, :stack S, :k K}`) is proposed as the canonical exchange format for network-transparent continuations. Because this lowered representation resolves execution-order ambiguity inherent in the Universal AST, it provides a simpler target for specialized execution engines (e.g., WebAssembly, LLVM, hardware FPGA) to participate in the `datom.world` ecosystem.\n     7\t\n     8\tTo safely \"lift on park\" and \"lower on resume\" across heterogeneous boundaries without violating host isolation or concurrency invariants, the following contracts must be fully specified before this feature is accepted:\n     9\t\n    10\t1. **Safepoint Reconstruction Metadata:** `:yin.code/source` mappings are insufficient for state reconstruction. The compiler must generate exact safepoint metadata tying canonical PC locations to physical register/stack mappings so that arbitrary hardware states can be predictably lifted into the canonical CESK format.\n    11\t2. **Recursive Portable Encoding:** Section 1.1 permits host functions and local stream handles in the environment. A recursive serialization protocol is required to encode these local references into portable descriptors, with explicit failure modes for un-serializable resources.\n    12\t3. **Dependency Closure & Context:** A parked fragment is not a complete configuration. The format must explicitly declare its required primitives, loaded modules, and the global store state required for valid resumption.\n    13\t4. **Ownership Arbitration:** Emitting a continuation does not transfer ownership. The transport composition must define explicit arbitration events to prevent source-wakeup races and duplicate resumes by multiple readers.\n    14\t5. **Code Identity (Content Addressing):** Because tempids (`:segment 123`) are local to a single machine's database, continuations must refer to code via immutable, versioned semantic profiles (e.g., content hashing) to guarantee the receiving interpreter executes identical logic.\n    15\t\n    16\t---\n    17\t\n    18\t# Universal Continuation Format — protocol draft\n    19\t\n    20\tStatus: Phase 5 design draft, 2026-09-14. Subordinate to\n    21\t[`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md); builds on the Phase 0 contract in\n    22\t[`yin.vm.semantic.md`](./yin.vm.semantic.md) (§1–§4), the storage contract in\n    23\t[`dao.jing.md`](./dao.jing.md), the stream contract in\n    24\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md), and the lease vocabulary in\n    25\t[`dao.lease.md`](./dao.lease.md). The five blockers above are answered in\n    26\t§7.3–§7.7, one section each, in dependency order: code identity first, because\n    27\tevery other part names code.\n    28\t\n    29\tEvery sentence in §7.2–§7.9 is a rule of the proposed protocol. §7.10 records\n    30\twhat is deliberately left open. Nothing below is implemented; the warning at\n    31\tthe top stands until an implementation phase resolves each blocker with tests.\n    32\t\n    33\t## §7.1 Stance: a continuation is a value, not a session\n    34\t\n    35\tThe semantic VM already parks as pure data (`yin.vm.semantic.md` §3.5): a\n    36\twait entry or a `:parked-continuation` record is `{segment pc env stack k}`\n    37\tplus a reason and resource ids, with no handle and no closure attached. That\n    38\trecord is *locally* complete and *globally* meaningless: its segment is a\n    39\ttempid, its stream ids are keys into this VM's store, its environment may hold\n    40\thost functions, and nothing says which primitives it assumed.\n    41\t\n    42\tThe Universal Continuation Format (UCF) is the **lift** of that local record\n    43\tinto a self-describing value that any conforming engine can **lower** back\n    44\tinto its own registers, and the discipline that governs who may lower it.\n    45\tThree roles participate, and they are stream roles, not parties to a call:\n    46\t\n    47\t| Role | Does | Holds |\n    48\t|---|---|---|\n    49\t| **Emitter** | lifts a parked configuration into a UCF value and appends it to a medium | nothing, once appended |\n    50\t| **Carrier** | any stream, file, DHT, or socket that moves the value; sees opaque data | nothing |\n    51\t| **Resumer** | reads the value, satisfies its declared context, obtains custody, lowers, runs | custody, for the duration of a lease |\n    52\t\n    53\tTwo consequences are load-bearing. First, **a UCF value is content**: it has\n    54\ta content address under `dao.jing`, equal values converge on one address, and\n    55\tcarrying it twice is harmless (`dao.jing.md`, *Materialization rule*). Second,\n    56\t**a UCF value on a stream is not a claim**: a stream has no privileged reader\n    57\t(`datom.world.md`, *Streams*), so emission transfers nothing. Custody is\n    58\tarbitrated above the stream, by leases over datoms (§7.7), exactly where\n    59\t`dao.stream.md` *Explicitly Absent* says a take belongs.\n    60\t\n    61\tThe reference engine for every rule below is `yin.vm.semantic`. Its\n    62\tconfiguration ⟨C, E, S, K⟩ with C = ⟨seg, pc, val, St⟩ (`yin.vm.semantic.md`\n    63\t§4.1) is the **canonical CESK**. A foreign engine conforms by lifting into and\n    64\tlowering from this configuration, never by extending it.\n    65\t\n    66\t## §7.2 Vocabulary and envelope\n    67\t\n    68\tUCF facts live under `:yin.k/*` (K for the continuation component of CESK).\n    69\tA UCF value is one open map with the `:yin.k/type` dispatch key; a consumer\n    70\tignores qualified keys it does not understand, as for every DaoStream\n    71\tenvelope. The value must survive the host codec structurally unchanged: no\n    72\tfunction, host object, or live handle anywhere inside it (`dao.stream.md`,\n    73\t*Envelopes*).\n    74\t\n    75\t```clojure\n    76\t{:yin.k/type      :yin.k/continuation\n    77\t :yin.k/contract  {:yin.code/contract \"v2\" :yin.k/version 0}      ; §7.3.3\n    78\t :yin.k/id        :segment/sha256-…                               ; §7.3.4, content address of this map minus :yin.k/id\n    79\t :yin.k/origin    {:yin.k/parked-id :parked-3 :yin.k/emitter <attribution>}\n    80\t :yin.k/frame     {…}                                             ; §7.4  the canonical CESK at a safepoint\n    81\t :yin.k/values    {…}                                             ; §7.5  portable encodings, shared subvalues\n    82\t :yin.k/requires  {…}                                             ; §7.6  dependency closure\n    83\t :yin.k/store     {…}                                             ; §7.6  reachable store slice\n    84\t :yin.k/carried   [{…} …]                                         ; §7.6  optional: segments shipped inline\n    85\t :yin.k/policy    :yin.k/exclusive                                ; §7.7  or :yin.k/fork\n    86\t}\n    87\t```\n    88\t\n    89\tThe four parts answer four questions a resumer must be able to ask of the data\n    90\talone: *what code* (`:yin.k/requires`, `:yin.k/carried`, and every segment\n    91\taddress inside the frame), *what state* (`:yin.k/frame`, `:yin.k/values`,\n    92\t`:yin.k/store`), *what world* (`:yin.k/requires`), and *who may run it*\n    93\t(`:yin.k/policy`, with the custody facts of §7.7 on their own medium).\n    94\t\n    95\tOutcomes of every UCF operation are data, one map with `:yin.k/status`, in\n    96\tthe DaoStream result convention. The full algebra is in §7.9.\n    97\t\n    98\t## §7.3 Code identity — content-addressed semantic profiles (blocker 5)\n    99\t\n   100\t### 7.3.1 The problem restated as data\n   101\t\n   102\t`:segment 123` is an entity id minted by one transactor; another machine's\n   103\t`123` is different code or no code. A continuation naming code by tempid is\n   104\ttherefore not a value, it is a pointer. UCF names code by **what it is**.\n   105\t\n   106\t### 7.3.2 The canonical instruction stream and its address\n   107\t\n   108\tThe segment attribute `:yin.code/hash`, reserved in `yin.vm.semantic.md`\n   109\t§2.2, becomes required on any segment that a continuation may reference. Its\n   110\tvalue is the `dao.jing` segment address of the segment's **canonical\n   111\tinstruction stream**, defined as the result of this pure function over a\n   112\twell-formed batch (§2.6):\n   113\t\n   114\t1. Drop the segment entity's `:yin.code/derived-from` and every instruction's\n   115\t   `:yin.code/source`. Provenance is not semantics; two segments that execute\n   116\t   identically address identically however they were produced.\n   117\t2. Replace every `e` with its `:yin.code/pc`, and the segment entity's `e`\n   118\t   with `:segment`. Entity ids are a transport accident (§2.3); pcs are the\n   119\t   fact.\n   120\t3. Replace every `:yin.code/target` and `:yin.code/body` ref with the pc of\n   121\t   the instruction it resolves to. The loader already computes this map.\n   122\t4. Drop `t` and `m`; emit `[e a v]` triples.\n   123\t5. Sort by `[e a v]`. Hash with `dao.jing/segment-key` on the sorted vector.\n   124\t\n   125\t```clojure\n   126\t;; the worked segment of §2.7, canonical form (excerpt)\n   127\t[[:segment :yin.code/type :segment]\n   128\t [:segment :yin.code/length 14]\n   129\t [0 :yin.code/op :closure] [0 :yin.code/params [x]] [0 :yin.code/body 6]\n   130\t [1 :yin.code/op :push]\n   131\t …\n   132\t [4 :yin.code/op :call] [4 :yin.code/argc 1] [4 :yin.code/tail? false]\n   133\t …]\n   134\t;; :yin.code/hash = (dao.jing/segment-key <that vector>)\n   135\t;;                = :segment/sha256-3f1a…\n   136\t```\n   137\t\n   138\tThe address is a **semantic profile** of the segment: it fixes the opcode\n   139\tmnemonics, operands, and control graph and nothing else. Any engine holding\n   140\tdatoms that canonicalize to the same address holds identical logic by\n   141\tconstruction. Verification is one hash (`dao.jing.dht.md`, *integrity\n   142\tagainst untrusted peers for the cost of one hash*).\n   143\t\n   144\tThe canonical encoding beneath `segment-key` is transitional\n   145\t(`dao.jing.md`, *Open items*). UCF inherits that limit unchanged: addresses\n   146\tare portable exactly as far as `dao.jing` addresses are, and when the pinned\n   147\tbyte encoding lands, `:yin.code/hash` values change with every other minted\n   148\taddress. This is the correct dependency; UCF must not mint a second addressing\n   149\tscheme.\n   150\t\n   151\t### 7.3.3 The contract stamp\n   152\t\n   153\tAn address fixes *which* datoms; it does not fix what `:call` means. That is\n   154\tthe interpretation the loader and the step loop supply (§1.1, *Interpretation\n   155\tCreates Semantics*). UCF therefore carries, once per value, a **contract\n   156\tstamp**:\n   157\t\n   158\t```clojure\n   159\t:yin.k/contract {:yin.code/contract \"v2\"    ; the §2.4 opcode table and §2.6 rules, by name\n   160\t                 :yin.k/version    0}       ; this envelope's own version\n   161\t```\n   162\t\n   163\t`:yin.code/contract` names a published revision of the opcode table and\n   164\twell-formedness rules. A resumer whose loader implements a different revision\n   165\tdoes not guess: it returns `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` (§7.9). Adding an opcode\n   166\tis a new revision; a resumer on the old one cannot run code that uses it, and\n   167\tthe stamp is how it finds out before lowering rather than at an unknown\n   168\tinteger in `case`. Two revisions may be declared compatible only by a\n   169\tpublished statement in this document, never by an engine's own judgment.\n   170\t\n   171\t### 7.3.4 References to code inside a continuation\n   172\t\n   173\tEvery place the local record says `:segment <id>` — the control, each\n   174\t`:return` frame, each `:eval-call` and `:request-sent` frame, each closure's\n   175\t`:segment` — the UCF frame says `:yin.k/segment <address>`. Entry pcs and\n   176\tframe pcs are unchanged: a pc is meaningful relative to the canonical stream\n   177\tand is stable under everything §7.3.2 normalizes away.\n   178\t\n   179\tOn lowering, the resumer resolves each address in this order and stops at the\n   180\tfirst hit:\n   181\t\n   182\t1. its own `:code` map, which the loader now keys by address as well as by\n   183\t   local id (an address already holding a *different* image is impossible by\n   184\t   construction, so the §3.1 \"an id must never come to mean different code\"\n   185\t   rule becomes a hash check instead of a policy);\n   186\t2. `:yin.k/carried` in the value itself, verified by re-canonicalizing and\n   187\t   re-hashing before load;\n   188\t3. a `dao.jing` read by address, verified the same way.\n   189\t\n   190\tA miss is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the address (§7.6.4). A carried or\n   191\tfetched batch whose canonical form does not hash to the address it claims is\n   192\t`:yin.k/hash-mismatch`, and nothing from that value is loaded.\n   193\t\n   194\tThe value's own identity `:yin.k/id` is the `dao.jing` segment address of\n   195\tthe UCF map with `:yin.k/id` removed. It is what custody facts name (§7.7),\n   196\twhat a carrier may deduplicate on, and what a `dao.jing` intake pool\n   197\tmaterializes. Equal continuations converge; a continuation re-emitted after a\n   198\tretry is the same value.\n   199\t\n   200\t## §7.4 Safepoints — where a canonical configuration exists (blocker 1)\n   201\t\n   202\t### 7.4.1 Only safepoints lift\n   203\t\n   204\tThe linear machine's registers are canonical only *between* instructions.\n   205\tInside a `:call` that has popped its operands but not yet pushed a frame, or\n   206\tinside a superinstruction the loader fused (§6.4), no ⟨C, E, S, K⟩ exists\n   207\tthat both describes the state and is expressible in the §2.4 vocabulary.\n   208\tUCF resolves this by rule rather than by metadata: **a continuation may be\n   209\tlifted only at a safepoint**, and a safepoint is a pc at which the reference\n   210\tmachine's transition has fully completed and its successor is the next\n   211\tinstruction to fetch.\n   212\t\n   213\tEvery parking transition of the reference machine already lands on one. The\n   214\tsafepoint set of a segment is closed and computable from the datoms:\n   215\t\n   216\t| Safepoint kind | Instruction | Canonical resume pc | Accumulator on resume |\n   217\t|---|---|---|---|\n   218\t| explicit park | `:park` | `pc+1` | the resume value |\n   219\t| blocked read | `:stream-next` (outcome `blocked`) | `pc+1` | the read value, `nil` on `end`, `:dao.stream/gap` on gap |\n   220\t| blocked write | `:stream-put` (outcome `full`) | `pc+1` | the written value |\n   221\t| foreign call | `:ffi-call` | `pc+1` | the correlated response value |\n   222\t| reification | `:current-continuation` | `pc+1` | whatever the resumer supplies |\n   223\t| halt | `:halt`, `:return` with empty K | — | not resumable; `:yin.k/type :yin.k/result` instead |\n   224\t\n   225\tThe `:yin.k/frame` is exactly the wait-entry shape of `yin.vm.semantic.md`\n   226\t§3.5, with segments replaced by addresses and values by their portable\n   227\tencodings (§7.5):\n   228\t\n   229\t```clojure\n   230\t:yin.k/frame\n   231\t{:yin.k/segment  :segment/sha256-3f1a…\n   232\t :yin.k/pc       5                         ; the resume pc, already pc+1\n   233\t :yin.k/reason   :next                     ; :park | :next | :put | :ffi | :reify\n   234\t :yin.k/val      nil                       ; accumulator; nil except for :reify, where the emitter may prefill\n   235\t :yin.k/stack    [ … ]                     ; St, operands in order, encoded\n   236\t :yin.k/env      { sym → encoded value }   ; E\n   237\t :yin.k/k        [ {:yin.k/frame-type :return\n   238\t                    :yin.k/segment :segment/sha256-…\n   239\t                    :yin.k/pc 5 :yin.k/env {…} :yin.k/stack-base 0} … ]   ; K, innermost last\n   240\t :yin.k/pending  {…}}                      ; the wait it was in, §7.4.3\n   241\t```\n   242\t\n   243\t### 7.4.2 Safepoint metadata for foreign engines\n   244\t\n   245\tThe reference machine needs no metadata because its registers *are* the\n   246\tcanonical configuration. A foreign engine — a register allocator over the\n   247\timage, a WebAssembly lowering, a JIT — keeps its state in physical locations\n   248\tof its own choosing, and must be able to produce the table above at every\n   249\tsafepoint. UCF requires such an engine to publish, **per segment and per\n   250\tengine profile**, a safepoint map it derives from the canonical stream at its\n   251\town load time:\n   252\t\n   253\t```clojure\n   254\t[[sp :yin.safepoint/segment   :segment/sha256-3f1a…]   ; the code, by address\n   255\t [sp :yin.safepoint/engine    :wasm32/v1]              ; the engine profile that produced this layout\n   256\t [sp :yin.safepoint/pc        5]                       ; canonical resume pc\n   257\t [sp :yin.safepoint/stack-depth 1]                     ; |St| at this pc, from the linearizer's stack discipline\n   258\t [sp :yin.safepoint/env-names  [x]]                    ; lexical names live here, when lexical addressing is on (§6.4)\n   259\t [sp :yin.safepoint/layout    {…}]]                    ; engine-owned: physical slot → canonical slot, opaque to everyone else\n   260\t```\n   261\t\n   262\tRules:\n   263\t\n   264\t- `:yin.safepoint/stack-depth` and `:yin.safepoint/env-names` are **derived\n   265\t  from the canonical stream alone**, by the same static walk the linearizer\n   266\t  already performs to place `:push` and `:call` (§5.3). They are therefore\n   267\t  identical for every engine and may be checked against the reference machine\n   268\t  by a conformance test that runs the segment to each safepoint and compares\n   269\t  `|St|` and `(keys E)`.\n   270\t- `:yin.safepoint/layout` is opaque to the protocol. It is a memo the engine\n   271\t  keeps for itself so that its `lift` is a table lookup. It never travels in a\n   272\t  UCF value; a resumer that is a different engine has its own.\n   273\t- `lift(engine-state, layout) → canonical frame` and\n   274\t  `lower(canonical frame, layout) → engine-state` are total on safepoints and\n   275\t  undefined elsewhere. The conformance obligation is `lift ∘ lower = id` on\n   276\t  canonical frames, tested against the reference machine's own frames.\n   277\t- A request to lift at a pc that is not a safepoint of the segment returns\n   278\t  `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` naming the pc. An engine that wants finer\n   279\t  interruption emits a `:park` in its own lowering; it may not invent\n   280\t  safepoints the datoms do not have.\n   281\t\n   282\tSafepoints are datoms, so a query can ask \"where can this program be\n   283\tmigrated?\" without running it, and a debugger's breakpoint set is a subset of\n   284\tthe safepoint set by construction.\n   285\t\n   286\t### 7.4.3 The pending wait travels as data\n   287\t\n   288\tA parked machine was waiting for something. That something is a resource of\n   289\tthe emitter's host, and the resumer cannot poll it. `:yin.k/pending` records\n   290\tthe wait in resource-independent terms so the resumer can re-establish an\n   291\t*equivalent* wait, or decide it cannot:\n   292\t\n   293\t```clojure\n   294\t;; a blocked :stream-next\n   295\t{:yin.k/reason :next\n   296\t :yin.k/stream {:dao.stream/identity <id> :dao.stream/descriptor <desc>}   ; §7.5, from `descriptor` on the handle\n   297\t :yin.k/cursor <opaque cursor value>}                                       ; a position, not a claim\n   298\t;; a sent FFI call awaiting its response\n   299\t{:yin.k/reason :ffi\n   300\t :yin.k/call-id :call-7                                                     ; the :dao.stream.apply/id\n   301\t :yin.k/call-out {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}}\n   302\t;; a retained (unsent) FFI request\n   303\t{:yin.k/reason :ffi-request-sent\n   304\t :yin.k/request {:dao.stream.apply/id … :dao.stream.apply/op … :dao.stream.apply/args […]}}\n   305\t```\n   306\t\n   307\tOn resume the resumer attaches to the descriptor and re-mints a wait entry in\n   308\tits own wait set. A cursor that fell behind receives `gap` on the first poll,\n   309\twhich is the honest outcome the contract already promises to a kept cursor\n   310\t(`dao.stream.md`, *Retention and Gaps*). An attachment that resolves to\n   311\t`not-found` is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with the stream identity, not a silent\n   312\t`nil`.\n   313\t\n   314\t## §7.5 Recursive portable encoding (blocker 2)\n   315\t\n   316\t### 7.5.1 The value classes\n   317\t\n   318\tEncoding is one total function `encode : value × path → outcome`, applied\n   319\trecursively to every value reachable from the frame: accumulator, operand\n   320\tstack, environment, every frame in K, and the store slice of §7.6. The\n   321\toutcome is either the portable form or a refusal naming the path. The classes\n   322\tare closed:\n   323\t\n   324\t| Value class | Recognized by | Portable form |\n   325\t|---|---|---|\n   326\t| scalar | nil, boolean, number, string, keyword, symbol | itself |\n   327\t| collection | vector, list, map, set | same shape, elements encoded |\n   328\t| closure | `{:type :closure …}` (§2.4) | `{:yin.k/closure {:yin.k/segment addr :yin.k/entry pc :yin.k/params […] :yin.k/env {encoded}}}` |\n   329\t| reified continuation | `{:type :reified-continuation …}` | a nested `:yin.k/frame` (§7.4.1), recursively |\n   330\t| parked continuation | `{:type :parked-continuation …}` | a nested `:yin.k/frame` with its `:yin.k/parked-id` |\n   331\t| primitive | a host function resolved by `resolve-var` from `:primitives` | `{:yin.k/primitive sym}` — the name, never the function |\n   332\t| stream handle | `{:type :stream-ref :id k}` store key, or a live v2 handle | `{:yin.k/stream {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}}` via `descriptor` |\n   333\t| cursor | `{:type :cursor-ref :id k}` store key, or a cursor value | `{:yin.k/cursor {:dao.stream/identity … :yin.k/position <opaque>}}` |\n   334\t| host object | anything else | **refused**: `:yin.k/non-portable` |\n   335\t\n   336\tThe primitive case is the interesting one. §1.1 admits host functions into\n   337\tthe environment because `:var` resolves a primitive symbol to the function\n   338\tvalue and a program may then bind it (`(def f +)`). At lift time the emitter\n   339\trecognizes such a value by **reverse lookup in its own `:primitives` map**\n   340\t(identity on the function object) and encodes the symbol. A host function\n   341\tthat is not in `:primitives` — one a bridge handler returned, one a module\n   342\tconstructed — has no name and is refused. The rule is *a function is portable\n   343\tiff it is nameable in the emitter's declared primitive set*, and the name goes\n   344\tinto `:yin.k/requires` (§7.6) so the resumer checks it has the same one.\n   345\t\n   346\tA stream handle is portable iff its transport produces a descriptor whose\n   347\t`attach!` can succeed somewhere other than the emitter's host. The encoder\n   348\tcannot know that; it encodes the descriptor and lets the resumer's `attach!`\n   349\treport `not-found` as data. What the encoder *can* refuse is a handle whose\n   350\t`descriptor` call fails, which cannot happen under the contract, and a raw\n   351\tin-memory handle admitted by an in-process stream's admission declaration\n   352\t(`dao.stream.md`, *Creation and Attachment*), which it treats as a host\n   353\tobject.\n   354\t\n   355\t### 7.5.2 Sharing, cycles, and size\n   356\t\n   357\tA configuration is a DAG, not a tree: a closure's captured environment is the\n   358\tframe's environment; a value on the stack is also bound in `E`. Encoding\n   359\tnaively duplicates, and a deep K makes duplication quadratic. The encoder\n   360\ttherefore builds a **value table**:\n   361\t\n   362\t- On encountering a collection, closure, or continuation it has already\n   363\t  encoded (host identity during one lift), it emits `{:yin.k/ref addr}` where\n   364\t  `addr` is the `dao.jing` address of the encoded subvalue, and places the\n   365\t  subvalue in `:yin.k/values {addr → encoded}`.\n   366\t- A subvalue is placed in the table when it is referenced more than once or\n   367\t  its encoded size exceeds a composition-supplied threshold; otherwise it\n   368\t  stays inline. Both choices yield equal semantics; only the table is needed\n   369\t  for correctness of sharing.\n   370\t- A cycle (a value reachable from itself through host identity) is refused\n   371\t  as `:yin.k/cyclic` naming the path. The reference machine cannot produce\n   372\t  one — `E` is extended by `merge`, never mutated — so the refusal is a guard\n   373\t  against foreign engines and bridge-returned host structures, not a case the\n   374\t  corpus hits.\n   375\t\n   376\tBecause table entries are content-addressed, a carrier that already holds an\n   377\tentry (a `dao.jing` intake pool, a DHT replica) need not carry it again, and\n   378\ttwo continuations parked from the same closure share its encoding. This is\n   379\t`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §6.3's checkpoint-as-published-fold applied to\n   380\ta single frame.\n   381\t\n   382\t### 7.5.3 Failure is total and names its place\n   383\t\n   384\tEncoding a continuation with one refused leaf refuses the whole\n   385\tcontinuation:\n   386\t\n   387\t```clojure\n   388\t{:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable\n   389\t :yin.k/path   [:yin.k/env 'conn]         ; where in the frame\n   390\t :yin.k/kind   :host-object               ; :host-object | :unnamed-function | :cyclic | :in-memory-handle\n   391\t :yin.k/hint   \"java.net.Socket\"}         ; classified, never the object\n   392\t```\n   393\t\n   394\tThere is no partial UCF value and no placeholder for a missing leaf. A\n   395\tprogram that wishes to be migratable keeps host resources behind stream\n   396\tdescriptors and named primitives, which is the same discipline the host\n   397\tboundary rules already impose on portable code (`datom.world.md`, *Host\n   398\tBoundaries*). The refusal is the emitter's outcome, returned as data to\n   399\twhatever asked it to lift; the machine itself is unaffected and remains\n   400\tparked locally.\n   401\t\n   402\t## §7.6 Dependency closure and context (blocker 3)\n   403\t\n   404\t### 7.6.1 Declared, not discovered\n   405\t\n   406\tA resumer must be able to decide *before lowering* whether it can run the\n   407\tvalue. UCF carries that decision's inputs explicitly:\n   408\t\n   409\t```clojure\n   410\t:yin.k/requires\n   411\t{:yin.k/segments   #{:segment/sha256-3f1a… :segment/sha256-9c02…}   ; every address in frame, k, closures, values\n   412\t :yin.k/primitives #{+ - < println}                                 ; every {:yin.k/primitive sym} encoded, plus every free :var in the segments\n   413\t :yin.k/modules    {my.mod :segment/sha256-…}                       ; module name → content address of its published manifest\n   414\t :yin.k/effects    #{:stream/next :stream/put :stream/make}         ; effect kinds the segments can raise\n   415\t :yin.k/ffi-ops    #{:op/add}                                       ; :yin.code/ffi-op values present\n   416\t :yin.k/streams    #{<identity> …}}                                 ; logical-stream identities referenced anywhere\n   417\t```\n   418\t\n   419\tEvery set is computed, not hand-written: segments and streams by walking the\n   420\tencoded value; primitives and effects by walking the instruction datoms of\n   421\tevery required segment (a `:var` whose symbol is not bound in `E`, the store\n   422\tslice, or a required module is a required primitive; each `:stream-*`,\n   423\t`:ffi-call`, `:gensym`, `:store-*` op names its effect). Because the\n   424\tcomputation is over datoms, the same query answers \"what does this program\n   425\tneed?\" for a segment that has never run.\n   426\t\n   427\t### 7.6.2 The store slice\n   428\t\n   429\tThe store S holds `def` results, stream handles, cursor entries, and the FFI\n   430\tpair (§4.1). None of it is global in the UCF sense; it is the emitter's. A\n   431\tcontinuation carries the **reachable slice**: every store key that a `:var`\n   432\tin any required segment could resolve to, or that a `:stream-ref`/`:cursor-ref`\n   433\tin the frame names, with values encoded by §7.5:\n   434\t\n   435\t```clojure\n   436\t:yin.k/store {counter 41\n   437\t              'log {:yin.k/stream {…}}}\n   438\t```\n   439\t\n   440\tThe FFI pair is never in the slice; a resumer has its own pair, and a pending\n   441\tcall travels through `:yin.k/pending` (§7.4.3) with the call-out descriptor\n   442\tof the emitter, because the response will arrive there and nowhere else.\n   443\t\n   444\tUnreachable store entries do not travel. A resumer's own store is merged\n   445\t*under* the slice on lowering: the slice wins on collision, because it is\n   446\twhat the program observed last. Anything the program did not observe is not\n   447\tthe program's state.\n   448\t\n   449\t### 7.6.3 Modules\n   450\t\n   451\t`:yin.k/modules` names each module by the content address of its published\n   452\tmanifest, on the model of `dao.space.index/publish-index!` (`dao.jing.md`,\n   453\t*Publication from an agent*). A module is satisfied when the resumer holds a\n   454\tmodule registry entry whose manifest address is equal; it is not satisfied by\n   455\ta same-named module of different content. Module *code* is segments and is\n   456\tcovered by `:yin.k/segments`; the manifest pins the effect handlers and\n   457\texports the module registry (`yin.vm.module`) consults.\n   458\t\n   459\t### 7.6.4 The satisfaction check\n   460\t\n   461\tBefore lowering, the resumer evaluates `:yin.k/requires` against what it\n   462\tholds and answers with one outcome:\n   463\t\n   464\t```clojure\n   465\t{:yin.k/status  :yin.k/unsatisfied\n   466\t :yin.k/missing {:yin.k/segments #{:segment/sha256-9c02…}\n   467\t                 :yin.k/primitives #{println}\n   468\t                 :yin.k/streams #{<identity>}}}   ; keys present only where something is missing\n   469\t```\n   470\t\n   471\tThe check is total and complete: it reports *everything* missing in one\n   472\tpass, so an operator or an orchestrating interpreter can act once. A missing\n   473\tsegment may be resolved by `dao.jing` fetch (§7.3.4); a missing primitive or\n   474\tmodule cannot be resolved by the resumer and is a routing decision for\n   475\twhoever chose this resumer. A resumer never lowers a partially satisfied\n   476\tvalue; a host with no implementation for something answers with the\n   477\tqualified unsupported outcome, which `datom.world.md` calls a correct\n   478\toutcome, not a gap to be filled.\n   479\t\n   480\t## §7.7 Ownership arbitration — custody as a lease over datoms (blocker 4)\n   481\t\n   482\t### 7.7.1 The race, named\n   483\t\n   484\tThe emitter parks, lifts, appends the UCF value to a medium, and keeps its\n   485\tlocal `:parked` record. Three readers see the value. The emitter's own wait\n   486\tset wakes because the stream it was waiting on delivered. Now four machines\n   487\tbelieve they may continue one computation. Nothing in DaoStream can stop\n   488\tthem: a stream has no privileged reader, there is no destructive read, and\n   489\t\"the hard part of a take was never the removal but the *exclusion* of\n   490\tcompeting takers, which is a lease over datoms\" (`dao.stream.md`,\n   491\t*Explicitly Absent*).\n   492\t\n   493\tUCF adopts that sentence as its design. **Resumption is a take. A take is a\n   494\twrite. Exclusion is a lease.** Nothing new is added to the stream contract,\n   495\tand no key is added to any result map.\n   496\t\n   497\t### 7.7.2 Custody facts\n   498\t\n   499\tCustody is negotiated on an **arbitration medium** the composition wires — a\n   500\t`dao.space` whose facts are ordinary datoms — using `dao.lease.md`'s\n   501\tvocabulary unchanged, with the continuation's content address as the leased\n   502\tsubject:\n   503\t\n   504\t| Step | Fact | Author | Keys |\n   505\t|---|---|---|---|\n   506\t| offer | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/offered` | emitter | `:yin.k/id`, `:yin.k/policy`, `:yin.k/medium` (identity of the carrier stream) |\n   507\t| candidacy | `:dao.lease/proposed` | a would-be resumer | `:dao.lease/subject = {:yin.k/id addr}`, `:dao.lease/proposal`, `:dao.lease/duration` (an ask) |\n   508\t| grant | `:dao.lease/accepted` | grantor | `:dao.lease/lease`, `:dao.lease/holder`, `:dao.lease/duration`, `:dao.lease/max` |\n   509\t| refusal | `:dao.lease/rejected` | grantor | `:dao.lease/proposal` |\n   510\t| tenure | `:dao.lease/renewal` | holder | `:dao.lease/lease` |\n   511\t| done | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/resumed`, then `:dao.lease/released` | holder | `:yin.k/id`, `:dao.lease/lease`, `:yin.k/result` — the address of the successor UCF value or of a `:yin.k/result` |\n   512\t| reclaim | `:dao.lease/lapsed` | grantor | `:dao.lease/lease`, `:dao.lease/cause` |\n   513\t\n   514\tThe two `:yin.k/custody` facts are the only additions to the lease\n   515\tvocabulary, dispatched on their own key so a lease judge reading the same\n   516\tmedium ignores them, and both are evidence, not authority: an offer creates no claim, and a `:resumed` record\n   517\tis a holder's report. Authority is exactly `dao.lease.md`'s: **only\n   518\tgrantor-authored facts establish terms.**\n   519\t\n   520\t### 7.7.3 The grantor is the possessing boundary\n   521\t\n   522\t`dao.lease.md` places the judge inside the boundary that possesses the\n   523\tresource. What is possessed here is the *right to run*, and UCF assigns it by\n   524\tpolicy carried in the value:\n   525\t\n   526\t- `:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive` — at most one incarnation at a time. The\n   527\t  grantor is the composition-designated arbiter for the carrier medium; by\n   528\t  default the emitter's host, since it minted the offer and can fence its own\n   529\t  wait set. The emitter is **an ordinary candidate**: it holds nothing until\n   530\t  it observes a grant to itself, and its local `:parked` record is\n   531\t  resumable only under that grant. This is the whole answer to the\n   532\t  source-wakeup race: the source does not wake on data, it wakes on custody.\n   533\t- `:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork` — anyone may resume; every resume is a new\n   534\t  incarnation. No lease is needed and none is proposed. Fork is a legitimate\n   535\t  semantics (speculation, map-over-hosts, replication), and it is declared\n   536\t  in the value so a reader cannot mistake a fork for a failed exclusion.\n   537\t\n   538\tA resumer that lowers an `:exclusive` value without an observed grant has\n   539\tlowered nothing the protocol recognizes; its incarnation is unfenced and its\n   540\teffects are discardable (§7.7.4). Conformance tests assert that a resumer\n   541\trefuses to lower without a grant, returning `:yin.k/not-holder`.\n   542\t\n   543\t### 7.7.4 Fencing: the lease id rides on every effect\n   544\t\n   545\tA lease bounds attention, not access (`dao.lease.md`, *The holder*). A holder\n   546\twhose lease lapsed in a partition may keep running for one duration plus\n   547\ttolerance, emitting effects the world must not honor twice. UCF fences at the\n   548\tonly place it can: **every effect a resumed incarnation emits carries\n   549\t`:yin.k/incarnation`**, the `:dao.lease/lease` value it runs under (or a\n   550\tfresh id under `:fork`), as a qualified key on the effect value and on any\n   551\tenvelope the effect's transport wraps it in. Consumers that must be\n   552\texactly-once — a `dao.space` transactor, an FFI callee with side effects —\n   553\tconsult the arbitration medium and drop effects whose incarnation has a\n   554\t`:lapsed` record. Consumers that do not care ignore the key, as the open-map\n   555\trule permits.\n   556\t\n   557\tThis is the same discipline as `dao.stream.apply` correlation ids: identity\n   558\tin the data, held by neither side. No callback tells a lapsed holder to stop;\n   559\tit stops at its own bound, and anything it did past the bound is\n   560\tdistinguishable after the fact.\n   561\t\n   562\t### 7.7.5 Successors and chains\n   563\t\n   564\tA resumed continuation runs to its next safepoint and either halts or parks\n   565\tagain. Parking again produces a new UCF value whose `:yin.k/origin` carries\n   566\tthe predecessor's `:yin.k/id` and the lease it ran under. The holder appends\n   567\tthe successor, records `:yin.k/resumed` with the successor's address, and\n   568\treleases. The chain of `:yin.k/id`s linked by `:yin.k/origin` is the\n   569\tmigratory computation's history, queryable on the arbitration medium: where\n   570\tit ran, under which grant, what it became. A rollback is a re-offer of an\n   571\tearlier link; effects past that link need compensation, not recomputation\n   572\t(`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §6.4).\n   573\t\n   574\t### 7.7.6 Restart\n   575\t\n   576\tA grantor that lost its ledger reclaims and re-grants (`dao.lease.md`,\n   577\t*Restart*). Its inventory of possessed resources is the set of offered\n   578\t`:yin.k/id`s without a `:yin.k/resumed` record on the arbitration medium; its\n   579\trecoverable holder is the `:dao.lease/holder` of the last grant. Both are\n   580\tqueries, which is why custody lives on a `dao.space` and not on the carrier\n   581\tstream.\n   582\t\n   583\t## §7.8 Lifecycle: lift on park, lower on resume\n   584\t\n   585\t**Lift** (emitter side; composition-driven, never automatic on every park):\n   586\t\n   587\t1. The machine is parked or blocked with a local record `r`.\n   588\t2. `lift(vm, r)`: confirm `r` is at a safepoint (§7.4.1), else\n   589\t   `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; encode (§7.5), else the refusal; compute\n   590\t   `:yin.k/requires` and `:yin.k/store` (§7.6); stamp the contract (§7.3.3);\n   591\t   mint `:yin.k/id`.\n   592\t3. Append the value to the carrier medium; observe `:dao.stream/ok`.\n   593\t   Optionally append required segments to a `dao.jing` intake pool first, so\n   594\t   `:yin.k/carried` may be empty.\n   595\t4. Under `:exclusive`, append `:yin.k/offered` to the arbitration medium.\n   596\t   The local record stays in `:parked`; the emitter is now a candidate like\n   597\t   any other.\n   598\t\n   599\t**Lower** (resumer side):\n   600\t\n   601\t1. Read the value; check `:yin.k/contract`, else `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`.\n   602\t2. Verify `:yin.k/id` by re-hashing, else `:yin.k/hash-mismatch`.\n   603\t3. Evaluate `:yin.k/requires` (§7.6.4); fetch what may be fetched; else\n   604\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied`.\n   605\t4. Under `:exclusive`, propose, then observe the grant, else `:yin.k/not-holder`.\n   606\t5. Load every required segment by address, verifying each (§7.3.4).\n   607\t6. Decode values (§7.5), merge the store slice under the local store,\n   608\t   attach to every stream descriptor in the frame and pending wait\n   609\t   (§7.4.3); an attachment `not-found` is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` at this step.\n   610\t7. `lower(frame, layout)` into engine registers; re-mint the pending wait\n   611\t   entry in the local wait set; run.\n   612\t8. At the next safepoint or halt, produce the successor (§7.7.5), record,\n   613\t   release.\n   614\t\n   615\tNeither sequence contains a callback. Each is a step function a\n   616\tcomposition-supplied driver repeats, as for every other interpreter in the\n   617\tsystem.\n   618\t\n   619\t## §7.9 Outcome algebra\n   620\t\n   621\tEvery UCF operation returns one map with `:yin.k/status`; the set is closed\n   622\tand every non-`ok` outcome carries the data needed to act on it.\n   623\t\n   624\t| `:yin.k/status` | Raised by | Carries |\n   625\t|---|---|---|\n   626\t| `:yin.k/ok` | any | the value, or the lowered VM |\n   627\t| `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` | lift | `:yin.k/segment`, `:yin.k/pc` |\n   628\t| `:yin.k/non-portable` | lift | `:yin.k/path`, `:yin.k/kind`, `:yin.k/hint` |\n   629\t| `:yin.k/cyclic` | lift | `:yin.k/path` |\n   630\t| `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` | lower | `:yin.k/contract` expected and found |\n   631\t| `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` | lower | the address claimed and the address computed |\n   632\t| `:yin.k/unsatisfied` | lower | `:yin.k/missing` |\n   633\t| `:yin.k/not-holder` | lower | `:yin.k/id`, the lease state observed |\n   634\t\n   635\tTerminal stream outcomes met during lift or lower (`transport-error`,\n   636\t`closed`, `invalid-descriptor`) are surfaced unchanged inside the UCF outcome\n   637\tunder `:dao.stream/status`; UCF does not rename them.\n   638\t\n   639\t## §7.10 Invariant compliance\n   640\t\n   641\t| Invariant | How UCF honors it |\n   642\t|---|---|\n   643\t| No hidden global state | Every dependency is declared in `:yin.k/requires`; the store travels as an explicit slice; custody is facts on a named medium. |\n   644\t| No implicit control flow | Resume happens only at a safepoint the datoms name; the successor chain is explicit `:yin.k/origin` links. |\n   645\t| No callbacks | Lift and lower are step functions; custody is observed, never notified; a lapsed holder stops at its own bound. |\n   646\t| No shared mutable state | A UCF value is content-addressed and immutable; two resumers of one value under `:fork` diverge into distinct incarnations. |\n   647\t| No layer collapsing | Canonicalize, encode, arbitrate, load, execute are separate functions with data between them; the semantic VM's step loop is untouched. |\n   648\t| No assumed graphs | Sharing is an explicit value table keyed by address; code refs are pcs relative to an addressed stream; nothing is resolved by pointer. |\n   649\t\n   650\tAnd the axioms: the continuation is a stream value (1); its meaning is\n   651\tsupplied by the resumer's loader and contract stamp, not carried in it (2);\n   652\tits code and its state are datoms under `:yin.code/*` and `:yin.k/*` (3);\n   653\tand it pauses, travels, and resumes anywhere that holds what it declares (4).\n   654\t\n   655\t## §7.11 Open items for the implementation phase\n   656\t\n   657\tThe warning at the top of this file is retained until each of these is closed\n   658\twith a test in the parity or conformance suite.\n   659\t\n   660\t- **Canonical byte encoding.** `:yin.code/hash` and `:yin.k/id` are exactly\n   661\t  as portable as `dao.jing/segment-key`, which is transitional. Closing this\n   662\t  is `dao.jing`'s open item, not UCF's, but UCF cannot be declared\n   663\t  cross-host stable before it lands.\n   664\t- **Contract naming.** `:yin.code/contract \"v2\"` needs a published revision\n   665\t  history; the first implementation phase writes it into\n   666\t  `yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.4 beside the opcode table.\n   667\t- **Safepoint conformance harness.** A test that runs every corpus segment on\n   668\t  the reference machine to each safepoint, lifts, lowers into a fresh VM, and\n   669\t  checks the parity suite's result; the same harness is what a foreign engine\n   670\t  runs to claim conformance.\n   671\t- **Reverse primitive lookup.** Depends on `:primitives` values being\n   672\t  identity-stable host functions, true today on all three hosts; the\n   673\t  implementation must assert it at `create-vm`.\n   674\t- **Arbitration medium wiring.** Which `dao.space` carries custody facts,\n   675\t  and the attribution resolver `dao.lease.md` requires, are composition\n   676\t  duties; the first composition to ship UCF is `yin.repl.core`'s handoff\n   677\t  demo, and its wiring becomes the worked example.\n   678\t- **Effect fencing at consumers.** `:yin.k/incarnation` on effects is\n   679\t  specified; which consumers check it, and how a `dao.space` transactor\n   680\t  consults the arbitration medium without a callback, is a design item for\n   681\t  `dao.space.transactor.md`.\n   682\t- **Store slice reachability through modules.** A module effect handler may\n   683\t  read store keys the static walk cannot see; until modules publish their\n   684\t  store footprint in their manifest, a composition may declare extra keys.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
