{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target working; cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md; rg -n 'segment-key|encoding|Materialization|Explicitly Absent|Envelopes|Retention and Gaps|descriptor|dao.stream.apply/id|resolve-var|parked-continuation|primitives|stream-put|wait-set' docs/design/dao.jing.md docs/design/dao.stream.md src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc src/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"2026-09-14 19:03:02.062 xcodebuild[61739:4172013]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-09-14 19:03:02.351 xcodebuild[61739:4172012] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\npython3: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\npython3: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-PukevMOy' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-09-14 19:03:02.950 xcodebuild[61745:4172038]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-09-14 19:03:03.110 xcodebuild[61745:4172037] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\n# Code Review Summary for `working`\n\n## Executive Overview\n- **Total Files Changed:** 1\n- **Lines Added:** +3 | **Lines Deleted:** -11\n- **Test-to-Source Additions Ratio:** 0.0 (0 test lines vs 0 source lines)\n- **Overall Change Risk:** `LOW`\n\n### Included Commits\n- 1af3b73 feat(semantic-vm): Phase 4 Benchmarks and Default Evaluator\n- 352979f feat(semantic-vm): Phase 3 Streams, FFI, consumers and handoff demo\n- 2bf3977 feat(semantic-vm): Phase 1 Core Linear Interpreter & Pure-data wait sets\n- 2b4e081 feat(semantic-vm): Phase 2 AST Lowering Compiler\n- 2ce4451 docs: add §7 Universal Continuation Format as proposed feature\n\n## Automated Scan Findings\n✅ No critical security or code quality smells detected by automated scan.\n\n## File Churn & Risk Breakdown\n| File Path | Category | +Add | -Del | Risk |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md` | Documentation | +3 | -11 | `Low` |\n\n## Reviewer Focus & Recommendations\n# Code Review PR Checklist & Taxonomy\n\nWhen performing code reviews, evaluate changes using this structured checklist and severity taxonomy.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Finding Severity Taxonomy\n\n| Severity | Label | Definition | Examples |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **P0** | **Critical (Blocker)** | Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, production crashes, secret leakage. Must be fixed before merging. | Hardcoded tokens, unhandled NullPointer on critical path, SQL injection, broken auth check. |\n| **P1** | **High (Must Address)** | Logic bugs, breaking API changes, missing error handling on external calls, race conditions, lack of tests for complex logic. | Swallowed exceptions, broken retry loop, missing backward compatibility in API schema. |\n| **P2** | **Medium (Recommended)** | Performance inefficiencies, code duplication, sub-optimal data structures, non-idiomatic style. | Unnecessary DB query inside loop, missing index, missing parameter validation. |\n| **P3** | **Low (Nit / Suggestion)** | Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs, minor doc polish. | Variable naming clarity, typos in docstrings, cleaning leftover TODO comments. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Comprehensive Review Checklist\n\n### A. Logic & Correctness\n- [ ] **Edge Cases**: Does the code handle null/nil/undefined, empty collections, zero values, boundary numbers, and unexpected inputs?\n- [ ] **State & Concurrency**: Are shared state mutations thread-safe? Are locks acquired/released properly without deadlock risks?\n- [ ] **Control Flow**: Are all conditional branches covered? Do loops have guaranteed termination conditions?\n- [ ] **Error Propagation**: Are errors caught and logged/surfaced appropriately, rather than swallowed silently (`catch (Exception e) {}`)?\n\n### B. Security & Credentials\n- [ ] **No Hardcoded Secrets**: Are API keys, passwords, bearer tokens, or private keys excluded from code and config?\n- [ ] **Input Validation & Sanitization**: Are external inputs validated before processing or passing to system shells, DB queries, or HTML rendering?\n- [ ] **Authentication & Authorization**: Do endpoints verify user identity and check permissions before executing state changes?\n- [ ] **Safe Dependencies**: Are newly added packages pinned to trusted versions without known vulnerabilities?\n\n### C. Architecture & API Design\n- [ ] **Preserve API Contracts**: Are public method signatures, REST endpoints, and schema definitions backward compatible?\n- [ ] **Single Responsibility**: Are modules, classes, and functions focused on a clear, single responsibility?\n- [ ] **Coupling & Cohesion**: Is the new code properly modularized without introducing circular dependencies or leaky abstractions?\n- [ ] **Idempotence**: Are retry loops or state-changing operations safe to re-execute on transient failures?\n\n### D. Performance & Resource Efficiency\n- [ ] **N+1 Queries**: Are database or remote API calls executed in batches rather than inside loops?\n- [ ] **Resource Cleanup**: Are file handles, database connections, streams, and web sockets closed explicitly (or managed via try-with-resources / context managers)?\n- [ ] **Memory & Allocation**: Are large datasets streamed or paginated rather than loaded fully into memory at once?\n\n### E. Test Coverage & Quality\n- [ ] **Test Coverage**: Do new features or bug fixes include corresponding unit or integration tests?\n- [ ] **Test Quality**: Do tests verify actual assertions rather than just checking that code runs without throwing?\n- [ ] **Edge Case Tests**: Are failure modes, invalid inputs, and error handlers covered by unit tests?\n\n---\n\n## 3. Review Feedback Guidelines\n\n1. **Be Specific**: Always reference the exact file line number, function name, and context.\n2. **Explain the \"Why\"**: Don't just say \"change this\". Explain the risk or benefit (e.g., \"This could lead to a race condition if two threads call `transact!` simultaneously...\").\n3. **Provide Concrete Fixes**: Offer concrete, copy-pasteable diff snippets where appropriate.\n4. **Acknowledge Good Work**: Highlight well-crafted solutions, thorough tests, or clean refactorings.\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:12:  \"Return the request stream descriptor from an endpoint descriptor.\"\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:18:  \"Return the response stream descriptor from an endpoint descriptor.\"\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:24:  \"Create an endpoint descriptor from two stream descriptors.\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:27:    request-descriptor  - stream descriptor where callee reads requests\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:28:    response-descriptor - stream descriptor where caller reads responses\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:37:  [request-descriptor response-descriptor]\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:38:  {:dao.stream.apply/request request-descriptor,\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:39:   :dao.stream.apply/response response-descriptor})\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:51:    {:dao.stream.apply/id <id>\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:61:  {:dao.stream.apply/id id,\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:70:       (contains? value :dao.stream.apply/id)\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:78:  (:dao.stream.apply/id value))\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:97:    id    - opaque ID; must match request's :dao.stream.apply/id for routing\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:101:    {:dao.stream.apply/id <id> :dao.stream.apply/value <value>}\"\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:103:  {:dao.stream.apply/id id, :dao.stream.apply/value value})\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:110:       (contains? value :dao.stream.apply/id)\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:117:  (:dao.stream.apply/id value))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:23:   closure: `engine/check-wait-set` resolves handles from the store on every\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:57:   primitives     ; primitive operations map\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:64:   wait-set       ; vector of continuations waiting on a transport\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:112:  \"Restore a wait-set or ready-queue entry into machine registers.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:127:            :wait-set (conj (vec (or (:wait-set base) []))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:258:                       (engine/resolve-var E (:store vm) (:primitives vm)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:370:              ;; :stream-put — target popped from St, value in val (13)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:427:                         (update :wait-set (fnil conj [])\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:437:                         (update :wait-set (fnil conj [])\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:470:  (let [v' (engine/check-wait-set vm)]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:582:                       :stream-put [opcode]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:695:     :primitives    primitive operations map\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:716:                                    [:primitives :modules :make-stream :call-in\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc:6:   a unary attach capability and a portable DaoStream descriptor; `attach`\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc:44:  [attach! descriptor]\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc:45:  (let [result (attach! descriptor)\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc:50:                       :descriptor descriptor,\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc:55:                          {:descriptor descriptor,\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc:64:   `(attach attach! descriptor)` mints the cursor at `:dao.stream/oldest`\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc:66:   `(at