{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc | sed -n '148,230p;595,645p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '35,85p;200,330p;370,422p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md | sed -n '282,308p;650,674p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/code.cljc | head -200\"","aggregated_output":"   148\t                      (:k entry))))))\n   149\t\n   150\t\n   151\t(defn- call-park-entries\n   152\t  \"Wait-entry builders for an instruction whose effect may block. The\n   153\t   continuation of an effect is the machine state after the instruction:\n   154\t   {segment, pc+1, env, stack, k} plus the transport fields the engine\n   155\t   fills in (§3.5). `St` is the operand stack with the instruction's operands\n   156\t   already popped.\"\n   157\t  [seg pc E St K]\n   158\t  {:stream/put (fn [_state _effect result]\n   159\t                 {:segment seg, :pc (inc pc), :env E, :stack St, :k K,\n   160\t                  :reason :put,\n   161\t                  :stream-id (:stream-id result)})\n   162\t   :stream/next (fn [_state _effect result]\n   163\t                  {:segment seg, :pc (inc pc), :env E, :stack St, :k K,\n   164\t                   :reason :next,\n   165\t                   :cursor-ref (:cursor-ref result),\n   166\t                   :stream-id (:stream-id result)})})\n   167\t\n   168\t\n   169\t(defn- run-effect\n   170\t  \"Materialize the registers, dispatch one effect through the engine, and\n   171\t   normalize the answer for the loop: `{:continue [state value]}` to go on at\n   172\t   pc+1 with `value` in the accumulator, or `{:stop vm}` when the effect\n   173\t   parked and the machine is blocked.\n   174\t\n   175\t   No `:restore-fn` crosses this boundary. A parked entry is pure data and\n   176\t   the scheduler below resumes it explicitly, so nothing live is attached to\n   177\t   the wait set (§1.1).\"\n   178\t  [vm seg pc val St E K effect park-entry-fns]\n   179\t  (let [{:keys [state value blocked?]}\n   180\t        (engine/handle-effect (put-registers vm seg pc val St E K)\n   181\t                              effect\n   182\t                              (when park-entry-fns\n   183\t                                {:park-entry-fns park-entry-fns}))]\n   184\t    (if blocked?\n   185\t      {:stop (assoc state :control nil :k nil)}\n   186\t      {:continue [state value]})))\n   187\t\n   188\t\n   189\t(defn- apply-call\n   190\t  \"The §4.2 call transition, with the arguments already isolated from the\n   191\t   operand stack. Returns `{:goto [seg pc val St E K vm image]}` to continue,\n   192\t   `{:stop vm}` when the call blocked, or throws for a non-function.\n   193\t\n   194\t   A tail call grows neither K nor St: the frame the caller would have pushed\n   195\t   is the frame its own caller gave it. A primitive that yields an effect\n   196\t   parks with the continuation after the call site.\"\n   197\t  [code seg pc val St E K vm tail? f args]\n   198\t  (cond\n   199\t    (= :closure (:type f))\n   200\t    (let [E' (merge (:env f) (zipmap (:params f) args))\n   201\t          frame {:type :return, :segment seg, :pc (inc pc), :env E,\n   202\t                 :stack-base (count St)}\n   203\t          seg' (:segment f)]\n   204\t      {:goto [seg' (:entry f) val St E' (if tail? K (conj K frame))\n   205\t              vm (get code seg')]})\n   206\t    (fn? f)\n   207\t    (let [result (apply f args)]\n   208\t      (if (module/effect? result)\n   209\t        (let [{:keys [state value blocked?]}\n   210\t              (engine/handle-effect\n   211\t                (put-registers vm seg pc val St E K)\n   212\t                result\n   213\t                {:park-entry-fns (call-park-entries seg pc E St K)})]\n   214\t          (if blocked?\n   215\t            {:stop (assoc state :control nil :k nil)}\n   216\t            {:goto [seg (inc pc) value St E K state (get code seg)]}))\n   217\t        {:goto [seg (inc pc) result St E K vm (get code seg)]}))\n   218\t    :else (throw (ex-info \"Cannot apply non-function\" {:fn f}))))\n   219\t\n   220\t\n   221\t;; =============================================================================\n   222\t;; The hot loop\n   223\t;; =============================================================================\n   224\t\n   225\t(defn- vm-hot\n   226\t  \"Run loaded work with the registers in loop locals.\n   227\t\n   228\t   `fuel`, when non-nil, is a number of instructions to execute before\n   229\t   materializing: `step` supplies 1 and `run` supplies nil. The loop exits by\n   230\t   returning a record when the machine halts, parks, blocks on an effect, or\n   595\t       :length (get-in attrs [seg :yin.code/length]),\n   596\t       :code (mapv decode instructions)})))\n   597\t\n   598\t\n   599\t(defn- store-image\n   600\t  \"Register one decoded image under its segment id, returning the code map.\n   601\t\n   602\t   Segment identity is stable: continuations, frames, and closures name\n   603\t   segments by id, so an id must never come to mean different code. An\n   604\t   identical reload — a batch re-sent, a batch run twice — decodes to the\n   605\t   same image and is accepted. A different image under a live id is a\n   606\t   conflict, and loading it fails rather than silently redirecting every\n   607\t   continuation that names the id.\"\n   608\t  [code image]\n   609\t  (let [seg (:segment image)]\n   610\t    (if-let [existing (find code seg)]\n   611\t      (if (= (val existing) image)\n   612\t        code\n   613\t        (throw (ex-info \"Cannot load segment: the id already holds different code\"\n   614\t                        {:segment seg})))\n   615\t      (assoc code seg image))))\n   616\t\n   617\t\n   618\t(defn vm-load-program\n   619\t  \"Load one `:yin.code/*` batch: validate it, decode it, store the image under\n   620\t   `:code {segment-id image}`, and set control to `{:segment id :pc 0}`.\n   621\t\n   622\t   This is the loader a composition hands to `dao.stream.observer/\n   623\t   run-on-stream` beside `engine/ready-for-ingress?` and the VM's runner; a\n   624\t   composition whose program stream carries `:yin/*` AST datoms composes the\n   625\t   Phase 2 linearizer in front of it. Parked continuations survive a load, so\n   626\t   one segment can resume another's. A segment id already holding different\n   627\t   code is a load error; loading the same image again is accepted.\"\n   628\t  [vm datoms]\n   629\t  (let [image (load-image datoms)]\n   630\t    (assoc vm\n   631\t           :program (:segment image)\n   632\t           :code (store-image (:code vm) image)\n   633\t           :control {:segment (:segment image), :pc 0}\n   634\t           :stack []\n   635\t           :k nil\n   636\t           :halted? false\n   637\t           :blocked? false\n   638\t           :value nil)))\n   639\t\n   640\t\n   641\t;; =============================================================================\n   642\t;; SemanticVM Protocol Implementation\n   643\t;; =============================================================================\n   644\t\n   645\t(defn- vm-reset\n    35\t\n    36\t(defn- outcome\n    37\t  [result]\n    38\t  (:dao.stream/outcome result))\n    39\t\n    40\t\n    41\t(defn- fail\n    42\t  [message data]\n    43\t  (throw (ex-info message data)))\n    44\t\n    45\t\n    46\t(defn resolve-var\n    47\t  \"Look up a variable name: env -> store -> primitives -> module registry.\n    48\t\n    49\t   The registry is a value supplied by the composition, not a global.\"\n    50\t  [env store primitives registry name]\n    51\t  (if-let [pair (find env name)]\n    52\t    (val pair)\n    53\t    (if-let [pair (find store name)]\n    54\t      (val pair)\n    55\t      (if-let [pair (find primitives name)]\n    56\t        (val pair)\n    57\t        (if-let [resolved (when (namespace name)\n    58\t                            (module/resolve-module\n    59\t                              registry\n    60\t                              (symbol (str (namespace name)\n    61\t                                           \".\"\n    62\t                                           (clojure.core/name name)))))]\n    63\t          resolved\n    64\t          (fail (str \"Unable to resolve symbol: \" name \" in this context\")\n    65\t                {:symbol name}))))))\n    66\t\n    67\t\n    68\t(defn- gen-id\n    69\t  \"Generate a unique keyword ID from a prefix and counter value.\"\n    70\t  [prefix id-counter]\n    71\t  (keyword (str prefix \"-\" id-counter)))\n    72\t\n    73\t\n    74\t(defn vm-blocked?\n    75\t  \"Returns true if the VM is blocked.\"\n    76\t  [vm]\n    77\t  (boolean (:blocked? vm)))\n    78\t\n    79\t\n    80\t(defn vm-value\n    81\t  \"Returns the current value of the VM.\"\n    82\t  [vm]\n    83\t  (:value vm))\n    84\t\n    85\t\n   200\t  (let [stream-ref (:stream effect)\n   201\t        stream-id (:id stream-ref)\n   202\t        handle (get (:store state) stream-id)]\n   203\t    (when (nil? handle) (fail \"Invalid stream reference\" {:ref stream-ref}))\n   204\t    (let [cursor (vm/mint-oldest handle :stream/cursor)\n   205\t          cursor-data (vm/cursor-entry stream-id cursor)\n   206\t          new-store (assoc (:store state) id cursor-data)\n   207\t          cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref, :id id}]\n   208\t      [cursor-ref (assoc state :store new-store)])))\n   209\t\n   210\t\n   211\t(defn handle-next\n   212\t  \"Handle :stream/next. Total over the seven read outcomes.\n   213\t\n   214\t   `ok` advances the store cursor to the exact returned successor. `blocked`\n   215\t   parks. `end` yields nil, as v1 did. `gap` advances to the recovery cursor\n   216\t   and yields `:dao.stream/gap`, so a program that reads it learns values were\n   217\t   lost. The three terminal outcomes are errors.\"\n   218\t  [state effect]\n   219\t  (let [cursor-ref (:cursor effect)\n   220\t        cursor-id (:id cursor-ref)\n   221\t        store (:store state)\n   222\t        cursor-data (get store cursor-id)]\n   223\t    (when (nil? cursor-data) (fail \"Invalid cursor reference\" {:ref cursor-ref}))\n   224\t    (let [stream-id (:stream-id cursor-data)\n   225\t          handle (get store stream-id)]\n   226\t      (when (nil? handle)\n   227\t        (fail \"Stream not found for cursor\" {:stream-id stream-id}))\n   228\t      (let [result (stream/next handle (:cursor cursor-data))\n   229\t            o (outcome result)\n   230\t            advance (fn [state* value]\n   231\t                      {:value value,\n   232\t                       :state (assoc state*\n   233\t                                     :store\n   234\t                                     (assoc store\n   235\t                                            cursor-id\n   236\t                                            (assoc cursor-data\n   237\t                                                   :cursor\n   238\t                                                   (:dao.stream/cursor\n   239\t                                                     result))))})]\n   240\t        (case o\n   241\t          :dao.stream/ok (advance state (:dao.stream/value result))\n   242\t          :dao.stream/blocked {:park true,\n   243\t                               :cursor-ref cursor-ref,\n   244\t                               :stream-id stream-id,\n   245\t                               :state state}\n   246\t          :dao.stream/end {:value nil, :state state}\n   247\t          :dao.stream/gap (advance state :dao.stream/gap)\n   248\t          (fail \"Stream read failed\"\n   249\t                {:outcome o, :stream-id stream-id, :cursor-id cursor-id}))))))\n   250\t\n   251\t\n   252\t(defn handle-close\n   253\t  \"Handle :stream/close. `close!` is total over {ok} and wakes nothing: a\n   254\t   reader parked on this stream learns of the close from its own next `next`.\n   255\t   Returns {:state s'}.\"\n   256\t  [state effect]\n   257\t  (let [stream-ref (:stream effect)\n   258\t        stream-id (:id stream-ref)\n   259\t        handle (get (:store state) stream-id)]\n   260\t    (when (nil? handle) (fail \"Invalid stream reference\" {:ref stream-ref}))\n   261\t    (stream/close! handle)\n   262\t    {:state state}))\n   263\t\n   264\t\n   265\t(defn- augment-wait-entry\n   266\t  \"Resolve one wait entry to a live handle and an opaque cursor out of the\n   267\t   store. The cursor is re-resolved on every round even when an earlier round\n   268\t   baked a value into the entry: another waiter on the same cursor-ref may\n   269\t   have advanced the stored cursor since.\"\n   270\t  [store entry]\n   271\t  (if-let [cursor-ref (:cursor-ref entry)]\n   272\t    (let [cursor-data (get store (:id cursor-ref))]\n   273\t      (assoc entry\n   274\t             :stream (or (:stream entry)\n   275\t                         (get store (:stream-id cursor-data)))\n   276\t             :cursor (:cursor cursor-data)))\n   277\t    (if (and (not (:stream entry)) (:stream-id entry))\n   278\t      (assoc entry :stream (get store (:stream-id entry)))\n   279\t      entry)))\n   280\t\n   281\t\n   282\t(defn check-wait-set\n   283\t  \"Check wait-set entries against their transports.\n   284\t\n   285\t   Every entry is resolved to a live handle and an opaque cursor out of the\n   286\t   store before `dao.runtime` polls it — resolution happens per poll, so\n   287\t   the stored entry itself never carries a handle. There is no\n   288\t   transport-local waking to fall back from: this is the only mechanism.\n   289\t\n   290\t   Entries are polled one at a time in wait-set order, and a woken reader's\n   291\t   successor cursor is written back to the store before the next entry is\n   292\t   resolved. Waiters sharing a cursor-ref therefore read distinct values — the\n   293\t   value at the cursor wakes the first, its successor wakes the next — instead\n   294\t   of every waiter reading the value at the shared pre-poll cursor. An entry\n   295\t   that stays waiting is retained in its stored, resource-id form; the\n   296\t   resolved copy was for this poll only.\"\n   297\t  ([state] (check-wait-set state nil))\n   298\t  ([state restore-fn]\n   299\t   (let [wait-set (:wait-set state)]\n   300\t     (if (empty? wait-set)\n   301\t       state\n   302\t       (loop [remaining wait-set\n   303\t              v (assoc state :wait-set [])\n   304\t              woken []]\n   305\t         (if (empty? remaining)\n   306\t           (let [new-tasks (make-woken-run-queue-entries v woken restore-fn)]\n   307\t             (update v :ready-queue (fnil into []) new-tasks))\n   308\t           (let [entry (first remaining)\n   309\t                 augmented (augment-wait-entry (:store v) entry)\n   310\t                 ;; A singleton wait set makes the outcome below belong to\n   311\t                 ;; this entry alone.\n   312\t                 polled (rt/check-wait-set (assoc v\n   313\t                                                  :wait-set [augmented]\n   314\t                                                  :ready-queue []))\n   315\t                 raw (first (:ready-queue polled))\n   316\t                 ;; A woken reader's successor is stored before later entries\n   317\t                 ;; resolve, so a shared cursor-ref advances within the round.\n   318\t                 advance (when (and raw (:cursor-ref raw) (:cursor raw))\n   319\t                           {(:id (:cursor-ref raw))\n   320\t                            (assoc (get (:store polled) (:id (:cursor-ref raw)))\n   321\t                                   :cursor (:cursor raw))})]\n   322\t             (recur (rest remaining)\n   323\t                    (assoc polled\n   324\t                           :store (merge (:store polled) advance)\n   325\t                           :wait-set (into (:wait-set v)\n   326\t                                           (if raw (:wait-set polled) [entry]))\n   327\t                           :ready-queue (:ready-queue v))\n   328\t                    (if raw\n   329\t                      (conj woken {:entry raw,\n   330\t                                   :value (:value raw),\n   370\t            base (assoc state\n   371\t                        :ready-queue rest-queue\n   372\t                        :store (merge (:store state) (:store-updates entry))\n   373\t                        :blocked? false\n   374\t                        :halted? false)]\n   375\t        (if-let [terminal (adapter/terminal-resume-outcome\n   376\t                            entry (:status entry))]\n   377\t          (adapter/throw-terminal-resume! entry terminal)\n   378\t          (restore-fn base entry))))))\n   379\t\n   380\t\n   381\t(defn park-continuation\n   382\t  \"Add a parked continuation entry and halt the VM.\"\n   383\t  [state cont-fields]\n   384\t  (let [id-counter (or (:id-counter state) 0)\n   385\t        park-id (keyword (str \"parked-\" id-counter))\n   386\t        parked (merge {:type :parked-continuation, :id park-id} cont-fields)]\n   387\t    (-> state\n   388\t        (update :parked assoc park-id parked)\n   389\t        (assoc :value parked\n   390\t               :halted? true\n   391\t               :id-counter (inc id-counter))\n   392\t        (telemetry/emit-snapshot :park {:parked-id park-id}))))\n   393\t\n   394\t\n   395\t(defn resume-continuation\n   396\t  \"Restore state from a parked continuation.\"\n   397\t  [state parked-id resume-val restore-fn]\n   398\t  (if-let [parked (get-in state [:parked parked-id])]\n   399\t    (let [new-state (update state :parked dissoc parked-id)]\n   400\t      (-> (restore-fn new-state parked resume-val)\n   401\t          (telemetry/emit-snapshot :resume {:parked-id parked-id})))\n   402\t    (fail \"Cannot resume: parked continuation not found\"\n   403\t          {:parked-id parked-id})))\n   404\t\n   405\t\n   406\t(defn gensym\n   407\t  \"Generate a unique ID and return [id updated-state].\"\n   408\t  ([state] (gensym state \"id\"))\n   409\t  ([state prefix]\n   410\t   (let [id-counter (or (:id-counter state) 0)\n   411\t         id (gen-id prefix id-counter)]\n   412\t     [id (assoc state :id-counter (inc id-counter))])))\n   413\t\n   414\t\n   415\t(defn handle-stream-block\n   416\t  [result entry]\n   417\t  (if (:park result)\n   418\t    (let [entry (or entry\n   419\t                    (fail \"Parked entry required for blocking stream\"\n   420\t                          {:result result}))\n   421\t          new-state (-> (:state result)\n   422\t                        (rt/park-task entry)\n   282\t## §3. The semantic VM as a DaoStream v2 interpreter\n   283\t\n   284\t### 3.1 Consumption: observer, loader, image\n   285\t\n   286\tThe VM implements `IVM`; program ingress is exactly the walker's shape:\n   287\t\n   288\t- The host composes a program medium, attaches `yin.vm.stream-observer`\n   289\t  to it, and drives `observer/run-on-stream` with `engine/ready-for-ingress?`,\n   290\t  a loader, and `vm/run`. The observer owns the handle, the cursor, and gap\n   291\t  accounting. The VM holds no program stream.\n   292\t- The loader for this evaluator is `semantic/vm-load-program`: it accepts a\n   293\t  batch of `:yin.code/*` datoms, validates §2.6, builds the image, stores\n   294\t  it under `:code {segment-id image}`, and sets control to\n   295\t  `{:segment id :pc 0}`. Segment identity is stable: an id already holding\n   296\t  a different image is a load error, and an identical reload is accepted —\n   297\t  continuations name segments by id, so an id must never come to mean\n   298\t  different code. A composition that carries `:yin/*` AST datoms on\n   299\t  its program stream hands the observer `(comp semantic/vm-load-program\n   300\t  linearize/lower)` instead; the loader is composition-supplied, so no\n   301\t  evaluator learns which form travels. `yin.repl.core/make-session` is\n   302\t  where that choice is made.\n   303\t\n   304\tThe step loop is then: `(aget code pc)` → integer `case` → transition. No\n   305\thandle, no cursor, no `next` inside a segment.\n   306\t\n   307\t### 3.2 Why a branch is not a cursor move\n   308\t\n   650\t\n   651\t### 6.4 Relation to bytecode, and what is reserved\n   652\t\n   653\tThe loaded image *is* bytecode. The difference from a traditional bytecode\n   654\tVM is only the stored form (five datoms per instruction rather than a few\n   655\tbytes) and the load step that decodes it. Execution cost is therefore\n   656\tbytecode-class by construction, and queryability is not paid for on the hot\n   657\tpath at all — it is paid once at load. The historic 1.0–1.1x register/stack\n   658\tline in `cesk-space-optimization.md` is the target; the walker sits at\n   659\t1.32x on that workload, the old graph-walking semantic VM at 1.69x.\n   660\t\n   661\tReserved, additive, none required for Phase 1–3:\n   662\t\n   663\t- **Lexical addressing**: `:var` gains `:yin.code/depth`/`:yin.code/index`\n   664\t  when the linearizer proves the name lexically bound; frames become vectors;\n   665\t  only unbound names walk store → primitives → modules.\n   666\t- **Superinstructions**: `:push-const`, `:push-var` fused by the loader (not\n   667\t  the linearizer, so datoms stay minimal).\n   668\t- **Host-array operand stack** inside the hot loop, materialized to a vector\n   669\t  only at park points (\"immutability observable only at park points\",\n   670\t  streams note §3).\n   671\t- **Content-addressed segments** (`:yin.code/hash`) for dedup across VMs and\n   672\t  for verifying a shipped continuation against its code.\n   673\t\n   674\tPhase 4 measured it: see §8.\n     1\t(ns yin.vm.code\n     2\t  \"Well-formedness of linear executable datoms (`:yin.code/*`).\n     3\t\n     4\t   A segment is one batch: exactly one `:yin.code/type :segment` entity plus\n     5\t   its instruction entities, whose order is an explicit `:yin.code/pc` fact\n     6\t   (`docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md` §2). This namespace judges the shape of a\n     7\t   batch and nothing else. Decoding it into an image and executing that image\n     8\t   belong to the semantic VM; the mnemonic-to-opcode mapping is the loader's\n     9\t   interpretation, not a fact checked here.\")\n    10\t\n    11\t\n    12\t(def mnemonics\n    13\t  \"The instruction vocabulary of §2.4.\"\n    14\t  #{:const :var :closure :push :call :return :jump :branch-false :halt :gensym\n    15\t    :store-get :store-put :stream-make :stream-put :stream-cursor :stream-next\n    16\t    :stream-close :park :resume :current-continuation :ffi-call})\n    17\t\n    18\t\n    19\t(def terminators\n    20\t  \"Instructions after which control never reaches pc + 1.\"\n    21\t  #{:jump :return :halt})\n    22\t\n    23\t\n    24\t(def ^:private structural-attrs\n    25\t  #{:yin.code/segment :yin.code/pc :yin.code/op})\n    26\t\n    27\t\n    28\t(def ^:private ref-attrs\n    29\t  [:yin.code/target :yin.code/body])\n    30\t\n    31\t\n    32\t(def ^:private required-ref\n    33\t  {:jump :yin.code/target, :branch-false :yin.code/target, :closure :yin.code/body})\n    34\t\n    35\t\n    36\t(defn- count?\n    37\t  [x]\n    38\t  (and (integer? x) (not (neg? x))))\n    39\t\n    40\t\n    41\t(defn- defect\n    42\t  [rule entity]\n    43\t  {:rule rule, :entity entity})\n    44\t\n    45\t\n    46\t(defn- index-batch\n    47\t  \"Entity ids in order of first appearance, and each entity's attribute map.\n    48\t   A repeated single-valued attribute keeps its last value, as\n    49\t   `yin.vm/index-datoms` reads one.\"\n    50\t  [datoms]\n    51\t  (reduce (fn [[order attrs] [e a v]]\n    52\t            [(if (contains? attrs e) order (conj order e))\n    53\t             (assoc-in attrs [e a] v)])\n    54\t          [[] {}]\n    55\t          datoms))\n    56\t\n    57\t\n    58\t;; Each rule below takes the indexed batch and returns a defect or nil. Rules\n    59\t;; run in order and the first defect wins, so a rule may assume every earlier\n    60\t;; one held.\n    61\t\n    62\t(defn- one-segment\n    63\t  \"Rule 1. A batch with no segment entity names no entity; a batch with\n    64\t   several names the second.\"\n    65\t  [{:keys [segments]}]\n    66\t  (when-not (= 1 (count segments))\n    67\t    (defect :one-segment (second segments))))\n    68\t\n    69\t\n    70\t(defn- instruction-shape\n    71\t  \"§2.3: every instruction names this segment and carries a §2.4 op. The\n    72\t   later rules read both, so a batch that lacks them cannot be judged by them.\"\n    73\t  [{:keys [seg attrs instructions]}]\n    74\t  (some (fn [e]\n    75\t          (let [ia (get attrs e)]\n    76\t            (when-not (and (= seg (:yin.code/segment ia))\n    77\t                           (contains? mnemonics (:yin.code/op ia)))\n    78\t              (defect :instruction-shape e))))\n    79\t        instructions))\n    80\t\n    81\t\n    82\t(defn- dense-pcs\n    83\t  \"Rule 2. The first instruction whose pc is missing, out of range, or\n    84\t   already taken is named; a count short of the length names the segment.\"\n    85\t  [{:keys [seg attrs instructions]}]\n    86\t  (let [length (get-in attrs [seg :yin.code/length])]\n    87\t    (if-not (count? length)\n    88\t      (defect :dense-pcs seg)\n    89\t      (loop [es instructions\n    90\t             seen #{}]\n    91\t        (if-let [[e & more] (seq es)]\n    92\t          (let [pc (get-in attrs [e :yin.code/pc])]\n    93\t            (if (and (count? pc) (< pc length) (not (contains? seen pc)))\n    94\t              (recur more (conj seen pc))\n    95\t              (defect :dense-pcs e)))\n    96\t          (when-not (= length (count seen))\n    97\t            (defect :dense-pcs seg)))))))\n    98\t\n    99\t\n   100\t(defn- sorted-by-pc\n   101\t  \"Rule 3. The pcs are a permutation of 0 .. length-1 by rule 2, so the batch\n   102\t   is sorted exactly when each instruction's pc is its position among the\n   103\t   instructions.\"\n   104\t  [{:keys [attrs instructions]}]\n   105\t  (some (fn [[i e]]\n   106\t          (when-not (= i (get-in attrs [e :yin.code/pc]))\n   107\t            (defect :sorted-by-pc e)))\n   108\t        (map-indexed vector instructions)))\n   109\t\n   110\t\n   111\t(defn- dangling-target\n   112\t  \"Rule 4. A ref that is present must name an instruction of this batch, and\n   113\t   a `:jump`, `:branch-false`, or `:closure` without its ref resolves nowhere.\"\n   114\t  [{:keys [attrs instructions]}]\n   115\t  (let [instruction? (set instructions)]\n   116\t    (some (fn [e]\n   117\t            (let [ia (get attrs e)\n   118\t                  required (get required-ref (:yin.code/op ia))\n   119\t                  refs (cond-> (filterv #(contains? ia %) ref-attrs)\n   120\t                         required (conj required))]\n   121\t              (when-not (every? #(instruction? (get ia %)) refs)\n   122\t                (defect :dangling-target e))))\n   123\t          instructions)))\n   124\t\n   125\t\n   126\t(defn- missing-terminator\n   127\t  \"Rule 5. Blocks are delimited by labels (pc 0, targets, bodies) and by\n   128\t   terminators. A block that does not end in a terminator ends only because\n   129\t   its successor is labelled, which the rule allows, so the one instruction\n   130\t   that can violate it is the last: nothing labelled follows pc length-1. An\n   131\t   empty segment has no terminator at all and names the segment.\"\n   132\t  [{:keys [seg attrs instructions]}]\n   133\t  (if-let [e (peek instructions)]\n   134\t    (when-not (contains? terminators (get-in attrs [e :yin.code/op]))\n   135\t      (defect :missing-terminator e))\n   136\t    (defect :missing-terminator seg)))\n   137\t\n   138\t\n   139\t(defn- negative-argc\n   140\t  \"Rule 6.\"\n   141\t  [{:keys [attrs instructions]}]\n   142\t  (some (fn [e]\n   143\t          (let [ia (get attrs e)]\n   144\t            (when (and (contains? #{:call :ffi-call} (:yin.code/op ia))\n   145\t                       (not (count? (:yin.code/argc ia))))\n   146\t              (defect :negative-argc e))))\n   147\t        instructions))\n   148\t\n   149\t\n   150\t(def ^:private rules\n   151\t  [one-segment instruction-shape dense-pcs sorted-by-pc dangling-target\n   152\t   missing-terminator negative-argc])\n   153\t\n   154\t\n   155\t(defn well-formed?\n   156\t  \"Check one segment batch of `[e a v t m]` datoms against §2.6.\n   157\t\n   158\t   Returns nil when the batch is well formed, else the first defect as\n   159\t   `{:rule r :entity e}`, where r is one of `:one-segment`,\n   160\t   `:instruction-shape`, `:dense-pcs`, `:sorted-by-pc`, `:dangling-target`,\n   161\t   `:missing-terminator`, `:negative-argc`, and e is the offending entity\n   162\t   (nil when the defect is an absent segment).\n   163\t\n   164\t   An instruction is any non-segment entity carrying `:yin.code/segment`,\n   165\t   `:yin.code/pc`, or `:yin.code/op`; entities with none of them are not\n   166\t   judged.\"\n   167\t  [datoms]\n   168\t  (let [[order attrs] (index-batch datoms)\n   169\t        segments (filterv #(= :segment (get-in attrs [% :yin.code/type])) order)\n   170\t        segment? (set segments)\n   171\t        instructions (filterv (fn [e]\n   172\t                                (and (not (segment? e))\n   173\t                                     (some #(contains? (get attrs e) %)\n   174\t                                           structural-attrs)))\n   175\t                              order)\n   176\t        batch {:attrs attrs,\n   177\t               :segments segments,\n   178\t               :seg (first segments),\n   179\t               :instructions instructions}]\n   180\t    (some #(% batch) rules)))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.md | sed -n '143,195p;380,398p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '200,250p;515,526p;650,676p;759,785p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc | sed -n '36,102p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc | sed -n '40,122p'; rg -n ':yin.k/|:yin.safepoint/' src docs/design --glob '\"'!yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md'\"'; rg -n 'datom|:put|:value' src/cljc/dao/runtime.cljc | head -35; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '156,198p;430,460p'; nl -ba docs/design/datom.world.md | head -45\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.md | sed -n '143,195p;380,398p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '200,250p;515,526p;650,676p;759,785p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc | sed -n '36,102p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc | sed -n '40,122p'; rg -n ':yin.k/|:yin.safepoint/' src docs/design --glob '\"'!yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md'\"'; rg -n 'datom|:put|:value' src/cljc/dao/runtime.cljc | head -35; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '156,198p;430,460p'; nl -ba docs/design/datom.world.md | head -45\"","aggregated_output":"   143\t## Materialization rule\n   144\t\n   145\tThe materialization is an idempotent union of content-addressed values. If\n   146\t`H(x)` is the address of the canonical encoding of `x`, observing `x` means:\n   147\t\n   148\t```clojure\n   149\t(assoc-if-absent target (H x) x)\n   150\t```\n   151\t\n   152\tRe-observing the same value is a no-op. Observing equal values through\n   153\tdifferent pool streams is also a no-op after the first insertion. Consequently:\n   154\t\n   155\t- replay is safe;\n   156\t- pool streams may be consumed in any interleaving;\n   157\t- duplicate publication is harmless;\n   158\t- immutable content writes do not require cross-stream coordination; and\n   159\t- a materialization can be reconstructed by replaying the available pool\n   160\t  streams.\n   161\t\n   162\tThe implemented write is `dao.jing/materialize!`: it derives the address from\n   163\tthe payload alone (`segment-key`) and asks the backend's `:put-content-fn`\n   164\tfor an explicit verdict. A backend validates before it writes: the address must\n   165\tbe a segment address and must hash to the payload, else it throws and stores\n   166\tnothing. `:inserted` means the value is durably stored now;\n   167\t`:present` means an equal value is already stored there, in which case the\n   168\tstored value is read back and verified. A collision in which an existing\n   169\taddress contains different canonical bytes is an integrity failure, not an\n   170\toverwrite, and throws loudly. `nil` is a legal payload distinct from absence:\n   171\t`get` takes a caller-supplied not-found and returns stored `nil` as `nil`.\n   172\t\n   173\t### Canonical encoding\n   174\t\n   175\tContent addressing is meaningful only when equal supported values produce the\n   176\tsame bytes on every participating platform. Distinct values must address\n   177\tdistinctly, both across types (`42` and `\"42\"` do not collide) and within one\n   178\ttype (`{:a 1}` and `{:a 2}` do not). Minted addresses are readable EDN: the\n   179\tname never starts with a digit, and a key survives `pr-str` → `read-string`.\n   180\tThe canonical encoder is therefore part of the storage contract even though the\n   181\tmeaning of the encoded value is not.\n   182\t\n   183\tCanonicalization may understand representation-level structure such as maps,\n   184\tsets, numbers, strings, and byte arrays. It must not understand domain concepts\n   185\tsuch as datoms, index orders, manifests, or any notion of a root.\n   186\t\n   187\tThe target encoding is a canonical flat byte representation suitable for\n   188\tcross-platform hashing and in-place reading. The current implementation uses\n   189\tan order-normalized `pr-str` as a transitional encoder — deterministic and\n   190\torder-insensitive, but not yet the pinned canonical byte encoding. This is the\n   191\tfirst open item under *Open items and current limitations*.\n   192\t\n   193\t## Storage ignorance\n   194\t\n   195\tDaoJing assigns identity to opaque content but does not interpret the data\n   380\tenters an address or a stored value.\n   381\t\n   382\t**Content addressing is implemented, transitionally.** `content-hash` hashes\n   383\tthe order-normalized print of a value; `segment-key` mints\n   384\t`:segment/sha256-<hash>` addresses; `segment-address?` is the strict address\n   385\ttest the backend layer enforces. As recorded in *Canonical encoding*, the\n   386\tencoder is transitional until the pinned canonical byte encoding lands.\n   387\t\n   388\t## Open items and current limitations\n   389\t\n   390\t- **Canonical encoding.** The order-normalized `pr-str` encoder must be\n   391\t  replaced by a pinned, cross-platform canonical byte encoding. Until then,\n   392\t  content addresses are portable only between implementations sharing the\n   393\t  exact print rule; when the encoding lands, `content-hash`, `segment-key`,\n   394\t  and every minted address change together.\n   395\t- **Durable observer checkpoints / long-running runner.** `observer-state`\n   396\t  and `observe-step!` are single-step and in-process. The checkpoint records,\n   397\t  per member, the stream coordinate plus the transport-minted cursor; the\n   398\t  coordinate is operational and never enters an address (serializability TBD in\n   200\tCreation brings a new logical stream into existence. Attachment joins an\n   201\texisting one. They are separate operations with separate inputs.\n   202\t\n   203\t- `create!` consumes a creation specification and returns a handle on a\n   204\t  new logical stream. `descriptor` on that handle returns `{ok}` with both\n   205\t  `:dao.stream/descriptor`, the transport-specific reachability value, and\n   206\t  `:dao.stream/identity`, the transport-independent logical-stream identity.\n   207\t  The descriptor carries the same identity internally; the sibling projection\n   208\t  and its value inside `:dao.stream/descriptor` must be structurally equal.\n   209\t  Both projections are total and outlive any attachment, so `descriptor` has\n   210\t  no failure outcome.\n   211\t- `attach!` consumes a portable descriptor and returns a handle on an\n   212\t  attachment to the **same** logical stream the descriptor names. Attaching\n   213\t  never creates.\n   214\t- A creation specification says how to build a stream. A descriptor only\n   215\t  names one — never executable code, never a construction program, never a\n   216\t  request to load a plugin.\n   217\t\n   218\tStreams are values that can be sent through streams — up to a serialization\n   219\tboundary. An in-memory stream whose admission declaration permits host\n   220\treferences can carry a handle intact. A dynamically delivered handle travels\n   221\tas composition data together with its declared surface, for example\n   222\t`{:dao.stream/handle h :dao.stream/surface #{:reader :writer}}`; the receiving\n   223\tinterpreter uses only that declared surface, and attaching to what it already\n   224\tholds is meaningless. A composition may omit the wrapper only when the\n   225\treceiver's fixed contract already declares the surface. But a handle does not\n   226\tsurvive encoding, and any\n   227\ttransport that encodes its elements — a file as much as a network socket —\n   228\tis a serialization boundary. Descriptors exist for crossing those\n   229\tboundaries, where the live value cannot travel and a name must. (What a\n   230\ttransport can carry is transport-owned: handing an encoding transport a\n   231\tvalue it cannot encode is reported as data like any other outcome.)\n   232\t`attach!` therefore consumes only descriptors, and\n   233\tresolves them against the transport's inherent operational state — the\n   234\tstreams a network endpoint serves because serving them is what it is, the\n   235\tfiles a filesystem holds. There is no stream table in this contract: no\n   236\tregistry maps descriptors to live handles, so a descriptor arriving\n   237\tsomewhere with nothing behind it — a ring buffer's descriptor on any other\n   238\thost, or on its own host absent a directory some composition chose to keep —\n   239\thonestly resolves to `:dao.stream/not-found`.\n   240\t\n   241\t### Envelopes\n   242\t\n   243\tA **creation specification** is a map with one required key:\n   244\t\n   245\t- `:dao.stream/type` — a qualified keyword naming the transport. This is the\n   246\t  dispatch key.\n   247\t\n   248\tEvery other key is transport-owned, qualified under the transport's namespace\n   249\t(for example a ring buffer's capacity), and validated by the selected handler\n   250\t— generic validation checks only the envelope, and a handler that rejects its\n   515\t  construct cursor internals or fabricate positions — there is no `seek`. A\n   516\t  cursor's representation belongs to the transport.\n   517\t- A cursor binds to the logical stream, not to a handle: any handle on the\n   518\t  same logical stream accepts it, so a cursor handed to another interpreter\n   519\t  works with that interpreter's own handle. Whether a cursor survives\n   520\t  serialization to another host is transport-owned and TBD, like the\n   521\t  descriptor envelope.\n   522\t- A cursor carries the logical-stream identity, not a reachability descriptor.\n   523\t  `cursor-mismatch` compares that identity, so handles reached through distinct\n   524\t  descriptors for the same logical stream accept the same cursor.\n   525\t- Advancing is receiving a successor cursor from `next`. The stream never\n   526\t  advances a reader; the stream holds no reader positions at all. Dropping a\n   650\t## Retention and Gaps\n   651\t\n   652\tRetention and overflow behavior are transport-owned and form part of the\n   653\tstream or writer surface's declared nature; a locally created stream records\n   654\tthem through its creation specification. A ring buffer retains a bounded window and evicts\n   655\tthe oldest; a file or append-only log retains everything; a network send\n   656\tbuffer refuses transiently. Which outcomes a given stream can produce —\n   657\t`full` on append, `gap` on read — and whether its `full` is transient or\n   658\tpermanent follow from the transport's declared nature. DaoStream names the\n   659\toutcomes and standardizes no policy.\n   660\t\n   661\t**Eviction never waits on a reader.** Retention is the stream's own declared\n   662\tpolicy and applies whatever any reader is doing — a stream holds no reader\n   663\tpositions, so it cannot be held back by a slow one. A transport that deferred\n   664\teviction until every reader had passed a position would make one reader's\n   665\tabsence every other reader's unbounded growth: a single stalled or departed\n   666\treader would hold the stream open forever, and a hostile one could do it\n   667\tdeliberately. Bounded retention is bounded, and a cursor that falls behind\n   668\treceives a `gap`.\n   669\t\n   670\tWhen bounded retention evicts a value, a cursor still pointing at it gets the\n   671\tgap outcome from the Reading table. The stream reports honestly and decides\n   672\tnothing: an interpreter replaying a log may treat a gap as fatal; a live\n   673\ttelemetry viewer may adopt the returned cursor and continue. Silently\n   674\tskipping evicted history is forbidden — a reader holding a cursor across an\n   675\teviction is told it missed values. This is a promise to a **cursor**, not to a\n   676\thandle: it is precisely what a kept cursor is worth, and it is why a consumer\n   759\t## Explicitly Absent\n   760\t\n   761\tAbsent from the public surface, by derivation from the invariants:\n   762\t\n   763\t- **an ambient registry** — a namespace-global dispatch table, or registration\n   764\t  as a load-time side effect. Host dispatch is ordinary data the host owns.\n   765\t- **a `closed?` predicate** — a predicate answer is stale the moment it\n   766\t  returns; operation results are authoritative.\n   767\t- **destructive reads** — a read that removes what it observes makes one\n   768\t  reader's progress every other reader's data loss. It is coherent only while\n   769\t  exactly one observer exists; a forwarder, an indexer, or a DHT replica makes\n   770\t  it theft. Where the capability went, so no later plan reinvents it: **taking\n   771\t  is a write.** Removing a tuple from an append-only log is not a mutation but\n   772\t  an assertion that it no longer holds, which `dao.space` already expresses as\n   773\t  a retraction datom — Linda's `in` is observe-then-retract, not a stream\n   774\t  operation. And the hard part of a take was never the removal but the\n   775\t  *exclusion* of competing takers, which is a lease over datoms\n   776\t  (`dao.lease.md`), not something a stream can promise. v1's `drain-one!`\n   777\t  appeared to solve exclusion only because it ran in one process against a\n   778\t  local ring buffer.\n   779\t- **`seek`** — every valid cursor comes from the stream; anchored minting and\n   780\t  kept cursors cover repositioning.\n   781\t- **a blocking take** — no operation waits.\n   782\t- **waiter registration** — there is no readiness extension.\n   783\t- **throwing conveniences** — operational outcomes are data.\n   784\t- **a retention or completeness predicate** — retention is declared, through\n   785\t  the transport's nature and the creation specification. Asking a handle would\n    36\t;; =============================================================================\n    37\t;; Attachment\n    38\t;; =============================================================================\n    39\t\n    40\t(defn- attached-handle\n    41\t  \"Call the unary capability once and validate its answer, yielding the\n    42\t   handle both `attach` arities build on: a non-`ok` outcome and a handle\n    43\t   without the reader surface throw exactly as `attach` documents.\"\n    44\t  [attach! descriptor]\n    45\t  (let [result (attach! descriptor)\n    46\t        outcome (:dao.stream/outcome result)]\n    47\t    (if-not (= :dao.stream/ok outcome)\n    48\t      (throw (ex-info \"Stream attachment failed\"\n    49\t                      {:dao.stream/outcome outcome,\n    50\t                       :descriptor descriptor,\n    51\t                       :result result}))\n    52\t      (let [handle (:dao.stream/handle result)]\n    53\t        (if-not (stream/reader? handle)\n    54\t          (throw (ex-info \"Attached handle declares no reader surface\"\n    55\t                          {:descriptor descriptor,\n    56\t                           :surfaces (stream/declared-surfaces handle)}))\n    57\t          handle)))))\n    58\t\n    59\t\n    60\t(defn attach\n    61\t  \"Attach to an existing medium and return the initial observer state, or\n    62\t   throw.\n    63\t\n    64\t   `(attach attach! descriptor)` mints the cursor at `:dao.stream/oldest`\n    65\t   and returns `{:stream handle :cursor cursor :ingress-gaps 0}`.\n    66\t   `(attach attach! descriptor {:cursor c :ingress-gaps g})` re-attaches at\n    67\t   a kept cursor, returning `{:stream handle :cursor c :ingress-gaps g}`\n    68\t   without minting one: the composition resuming a session knows where its\n    69\t   previous one stopped, a cursor already covers repositioning, the\n    70\t   transport validates the kept cursor on the first `next`, and the gap\n    71\t   count is seeded from the checkpoint rather than reset to zero.\n    72\t\n    73\t   `attach!` is a unary attachment capability: `(attach! descriptor)`. It is\n    74\t   called exactly once and there is no fallback to stream creation. A\n    75\t   non-`ok` attachment or cursor outcome throws `ex-info` preserving the\n    76\t   original `:dao.stream/outcome`. A handle without the reader surface is a\n    77\t   host assembly error reported with the descriptor and the handle's declared\n    78\t   surfaces, without inventing a DaoStream outcome. Attachment cleanup stays\n    79\t   with the host: this function closes nothing, and a failure leaves no\n    80\t   partial observer behind.\"\n    81\t  ([attach! descriptor]\n    82\t   (let [handle (attached-handle attach! descriptor)\n    83\t         minted (stream/cursor handle stream/anchor-oldest)\n    84\t         mint-outcome (:dao.stream/outcome minted)]\n    85\t     (if-not (= :dao.stream/ok mint-outcome)\n    86\t       (throw (ex-info \"Observer cursor could not be minted\"\n    87\t                       {:dao.stream/outcome mint-outcome,\n    88\t                        :descriptor descriptor,\n    89\t                        :result minted}))\n    90\t       {:stream handle,\n    91\t        :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor minted),\n    92\t        :ingress-gaps 0})))\n    93\t  ([attach! descriptor {:keys [cursor ingress-gaps]}]\n    94\t   {:stream (attached-handle attach! descriptor),\n    95\t    :cursor cursor,\n    96\t    :ingress-gaps ingress-gaps}))\n    97\t\n    98\t\n    99\t;; =============================================================================\n   100\t;; Observation\n   101\t;; =============================================================================\n   102\t\n    40\t\n    41\t\n    42\t(defn request\n    43\t  \"Create a request datom.\n    44\t\n    45\t  Args:\n    46\t    id   - keyword or other opaque ID; must be present in response\n    47\t    op   - keyword naming the operation\n    48\t    args - vector of argument values\n    49\t\n    50\t  Returns:\n    51\t    {:dao.stream.apply/id <id>\n    52\t     :dao.stream.apply/op <op>\n    53\t     :dao.stream.apply/args <args>}\"\n    54\t  [id op args]\n    55\t  (when-not (keyword? op)\n    56\t    (throw (ex-info \"dao.stream.apply request op must be a keyword\"\n    57\t                    {:id id, :op op, :args args})))\n    58\t  (when-not (vector? args)\n    59\t    (throw (ex-info \"dao.stream.apply request args must be a vector\"\n    60\t                    {:id id, :op op, :args args})))\n    61\t  {:dao.stream.apply/id id,\n    62\t   :dao.stream.apply/op op,\n    63\t   :dao.stream.apply/args args})\n    64\t\n    65\t\n    66\t(defn request?\n    67\t  \"True when value has the mandatory dao.stream.apply request fields.\"\n    68\t  [value]\n    69\t  (and (map? value)\n    70\t       (contains? value :dao.stream.apply/id)\n    71\t       (keyword? (:dao.stream.apply/op value))\n    72\t       (vector? (:dao.stream.apply/args value))))\n    73\t\n    74\t\n    75\t(defn request-id\n    76\t  \"Return the opaque request id.\"\n    77\t  [value]\n    78\t  (:dao.stream.apply/id value))\n    79\t\n    80\t\n    81\t(defn request-op\n    82\t  \"Return the request operation keyword.\"\n    83\t  [value]\n    84\t  (:dao.stream.apply/op value))\n    85\t\n    86\t\n    87\t(defn request-args\n    88\t  \"Return the request argument vector.\"\n    89\t  [value]\n    90\t  (:dao.stream.apply/args value))\n    91\t\n    92\t\n    93\t(defn response\n    94\t  \"Create a response datom.\n    95\t\n    96\t  Args:\n    97\t    id    - opaque ID; must match request's :dao.stream.apply/id for routing\n    98\t    value - any value (result, error, etc.)\n    99\t\n   100\t  Returns:\n   101\t    {:dao.stream.apply/id <id> :dao.stream.apply/value <value>}\"\n   102\t  [id value]\n   103\t  {:dao.stream.apply/id id, :dao.stream.apply/value value})\n   104\t\n   105\t\n   106\t(defn response?\n   107\t  \"True when value has the mandatory dao.stream.apply response fields.\"\n   108\t  [value]\n   109\t  (and (map? value)\n   110\t       (contains? value :dao.stream.apply/id)\n   111\t       (contains? value :dao.stream.apply/value)))\n   112\t\n   113\t\n   114\t(defn response-id\n   115\t  \"Return the opaque response id.\"\n   116\t  [value]\n   117\t  (:dao.stream.apply/id value))\n   118\t\n   119\t\n   120\t(defn response-value\n   121\t  \"Return the response payload.\"\n   122\t  [value]\n13:     the next runtime state. Ready entries carry `:value`, `:status`, and\n15:     (`:next` or `:put`), `:stream` (a live v2 handle), and either `:cursor`\n16:     or `:datom`. The runtime resolves nothing: the caller that parks a task\n26:     `{:value nil :status :end}`, `gap` as `{:value :dao.stream/gap\n33:     `:put` — and moves resolved entries to the ready queue in wait-set\n37:   - **A parked writer retries by appending.** A `:put` entry re-attempts\n113:    :dao.stream/ok [:ready {:value (:dao.stream/value result),\n117:    :dao.stream/end [:ready {:value nil, :status :end}]\n121:    :dao.stream/gap [:ready {:value :dao.stream/gap,\n126:    [:ready {:value (:dao.stream/outcome result),\n130:    [:ready {:value (:dao.stream/outcome result),\n142:    :dao.stream/ok [:ready {:value val, :status :ok}]\n144:    :dao.stream/closed [:ready {:value nil, :status :end}]\n146:    [:ready {:value (:dao.stream/outcome result),\n148:    [:ready {:value (:dao.stream/outcome result),\n176:                  :put (write-outcome->task\n177:                         (stream/append! (:stream entry) (:datom entry))\n178:                         (:datom entry))\n194:   Returns {:state updated-rt :result result-tag :value v :cursor c}.\"\n210:       :value (:value updates),\n216:   Returns {:state updated-rt :result result-tag :value v}.\"\n225:                                       :reason :put\n227:                                       :datom val))\n231:       {:state rt, :result (:status updates), :value (:value updates)}))))\n259:            ((:resume entry) state' entry (:value entry))))))))\n   156\t\n   157\t(defn handle-make\n   158\t  \"Handle :stream/make. Creates a stream through the composition's\n   159\t   `:make-stream` and stores the handle.\n   160\t\n   161\t   A nil capacity has no v2 meaning, so the module path defaults exactly as\n   162\t   the AST path does. Absent a supplied constructor this fails and says so;\n   163\t   silently falling back to a private transport is the failure mode the rule\n   164\t   exists to prevent.\n   165\t   Returns [stream-ref updated-state].\"\n   166\t  [state effect id]\n   167\t  (let [capacity (or (:capacity effect) vm/default-stream-capacity)\n   168\t        handle (vm/create-stream! (:make-stream state) capacity :stream/make)\n   169\t        new-store (assoc (:store state) id handle)\n   170\t        stream-ref {:type :stream-ref, :id id}]\n   171\t    [stream-ref (assoc state :store new-store)]))\n   172\t\n   173\t\n   174\t(defn handle-put\n   175\t  \"Handle :stream/put. Total over the five append outcomes.\n   176\t   Returns {:value v :state s} on success, {:park true :stream-id id :state s}\n   177\t   on `full`. `closed`, `invalid-value` and `transport-error` are errors that\n   178\t   name their outcome, as v1's throw on a closed stream did.\"\n   179\t  [state effect]\n   180\t  (let [stream-ref (:stream effect)\n   181\t        val (:val effect)\n   182\t        stream-id (:id stream-ref)\n   183\t        handle (get (:store state) stream-id)]\n   184\t    (when (nil? handle) (fail \"Invalid stream reference\" {:ref stream-ref}))\n   185\t    (let [result (stream/append! handle val)\n   186\t          o (outcome result)]\n   187\t      (case o\n   188\t        :dao.stream/ok {:value val, :state state}\n   189\t        :dao.stream/full {:park true, :stream-id stream-id, :state state}\n   190\t        (fail \"Stream append failed\" {:outcome o, :stream-id stream-id})))))\n   191\t\n   192\t\n   193\t(defn handle-cursor\n   194\t  \"Handle :stream/cursor. Mints an opaque cursor at `:dao.stream/oldest`.\n   195\t\n   196\t   v1 fabricated `{:position 0}` and touched no stream. Minting is a stream\n   197\t   operation, so `closed` and `transport-error` arrive here.\n   198\t   Returns [cursor-ref updated-state].\"\n   430\t(defn handle-effect\n   431\t  \"Dispatch an effect and return {:state updated-state :value v :blocked? bool}.\n   432\t   park-entry-fns maps :stream/put and :stream/next to functions that build\n   433\t   wait entries.\n   434\t\n   435\t   A parked entry is stored as the builder left it plus, for a writer, the\n   436\t   `:datom` it retries: registers and resource ids only. Neither the live\n   437\t   stream handle nor a restore closure is attached — `check-wait-set`\n   438\t   resolves handles from the store at every poll, and the scheduler that pops\n   439\t   a woken entry dispatches restoration itself — so a blocked entry is pure\n   440\t   data and survives serialization.\"\n   441\t  [state effect {:keys [park-entry-fns], :as opts}]\n   442\t  (let [park-entry (get park-entry-fns (:effect effect))\n   443\t        result\n   444\t        (case (:effect effect)\n   445\t          :vm/store-put {:state (assoc state\n   446\t                                       :store (assoc (:store state)\n   447\t                                                     (:key effect)\n   448\t                                                     (:val effect))),\n   449\t                         :value (:val effect),\n   450\t                         :blocked? false}\n   451\t          :stream/make\n   452\t          (let [[id s'] (gensym state \"stream\")\n   453\t                [stream-ref new-state] (handle-make s' effect id)]\n   454\t            {:state new-state, :value stream-ref, :blocked? false})\n   455\t          :stream/cursor\n   456\t          (let [[id s'] (gensym state \"cursor\")\n   457\t                [cursor-ref new-state] (handle-cursor s' effect id)]\n   458\t            {:state new-state, :value cursor-ref, :blocked? false})\n   459\t          :stream/put\n   460\t          (let [result (handle-put state effect)]\n     1\t---\n     2\tdescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, fundamental primitives (tuples, streams), design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n     3\t---\n     4\t\n     5\t# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n     6\t\n     7\t## Core Philosophy\n     8\t\n     9\tEverything is data.\n    10\tInterpretation > abstraction.\n    11\tExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\n    12\tRestrictions are a feature.\n    13\t\n    14\t### Foundational Axioms\n    15\t\n    16\t1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams. Persistent facts are immutable tuples and datoms `[e a v t m]`.\n    17\t2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n    18\t3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n    19\t4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n    20\t\n    21\t## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n    22\t\n    23\t- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n    24\t- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n    25\t- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is events on a stream.\n    26\t- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n    27\t- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n    28\t- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n    29\t\n    30\t## Tuples and Datoms\n    31\t\n    32\tTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\n    33\tA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata).\n    34\tSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly. Streams carry whatever values the consumer needs.\n    35\t\n    36\tSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n    37\t\n    38\t## Streams\n    39\t\n    40\tAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\n    41\tNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\n    42\tSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\n    43\tStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\n    44\t\n    45\tA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
