{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '96,113p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc | sed -n '203,231p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '459,479p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc | sed -n '45,105p'\"","aggregated_output":"    96\t## Result Convention\n    97\t\n    98\tEvery DaoStream operation returns data.\n    99\t\n   100\t- Each operation's set of outcomes is **exhaustive**: a transport may produce\n   101\t  fewer of them, never any outside the set. Which subset it produces is part of\n   102\t  its declared nature (see Surfaces). An outcome is a map whose\n   103\t  `:dao.stream/outcome` key names the outcome with a qualified keyword. Every\n   104\t  operation returns such a map — including projections like `descriptor` and\n   105\t  `cursor`, whose success map carries the projected value.\n   106\t- `:dao.stream/outcome` is present in every result map and is never repeated\n   107\t  in the per-outcome tables below; the keys those tables name are required in\n   108\t  addition to it.\n   109\t- Result maps are **open**: the contract fixes which keys are required per\n   110\t  outcome, and a consumer must ignore keys it does not understand. This is\n   111\t  what allows future extensions (for example readiness notification) to be\n   112\t  added without breaking existing consumers.\n   113\t- Exceptions are reserved for defects in the host's own assembly of\n   203\t(defn content-hash\n   204\t  \"SHA-256 of the order-normalized print of v. Total over any value.\n   205\t\n   206\t   Transitional: order-normalized pr-str is the current encoder, NOT the\n   207\t   pinned cross-platform canonical byte encoding the spec calls for\n   208\t   (docs/design/dao.jing.md, Canonical encoding), which is still an open\n   209\t   migration item. Content addresses are portable only between\n   210\t   implementations sharing this exact print rule; when the canonical\n   211\t   encoding lands, content-hash, segment-key, and every minted address\n   212\t   change together.\"\n   213\t  [v]\n   214\t  (sha256 (order-normalized-print v)))\n   215\t\n   216\t\n   217\t(defn segment-key\n   218\t  \"Mint the content-addressed key for an opaque payload, derived solely from\n   219\t   the payload: :segment/sha256-<hash(payload)>. The algorithm prefix is\n   220\t   load-bearing twice over: it names the hash function, and it keeps the\n   221\t   keyword readable EDN — a name starting with a bare hex digit cannot\n   222\t   survive print -> read, which poisons every EDN boundary content\n   223\t   addresses cross.\"\n   224\t  [v]\n   225\t  (keyword \"segment\" (str \"sha256-\" (content-hash v))))\n   226\t\n   227\t\n   228\t(defn segment-address?\n   229\t  \"True when x is a content address of the form :segment/sha256-<64 hex>.\n   230\t   This is the only address class DaoJing reads or writes: arbitrary keys\n   231\t   and mutable roots are outside DaoJing.\"\n   459\t          :stream/put\n   460\t          (let [result (handle-put state effect)]\n   461\t            (if (:park result)\n   462\t              (let [built-entry (when park-entry\n   463\t                                  (park-entry state effect result))\n   464\t                    built-entry (if (and built-entry (not (:datom built-entry)))\n   465\t                                  (assoc built-entry :datom (:val effect))\n   466\t                                  built-entry)]\n   467\t                (handle-stream-block result built-entry))\n   468\t              {:state (:state result),\n   469\t               :value (:value result),\n   470\t               :blocked? false}))\n   471\t          :stream/next\n   472\t          (let [result (handle-next state effect)]\n   473\t            (if (:park result)\n   474\t              (let [built-entry (when park-entry\n   475\t                                  (park-entry state effect result))]\n   476\t                (handle-stream-block result built-entry))\n   477\t              {:state (:state result),\n   478\t               :value (:value result),\n   479\t               :blocked? false}))\n    45\t  [store what]\n    46\t  (or (call-pair store)\n    47\t      (throw (ex-info \"This VM was constructed without an FFI call pair, so it cannot make a dao.stream.apply call\"\n    48\t                      {:what what}))))\n    49\t\n    50\t\n    51\t(defn call-response-wait-entry\n    52\t  \"The polling wait entry for a sent call awaiting its correlated response.\"\n    53\t  [call-id next-k env]\n    54\t  {:k {:type :dao.stream.apply/eval-call,\n    55\t       :next next-k,\n    56\t       :env env,\n    57\t       :call-id call-id},\n    58\t   :env env,\n    59\t   :cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref, :id vm/call-out-cursor-key},\n    60\t   :reason :next,\n    61\t   :stream-id vm/call-out-stream-key})\n    62\t\n    63\t\n    64\t(defn call-result\n    65\t  \"Unwrap a response envelope for the continuation that made the call.\n    66\t\n    67\t   v1 read `:dao.stream.apply/value` off a response that could only succeed.\n    68\t   A v2 response carries exactly one of `ok` or `error`, and correlation is\n    69\t   checked here rather than assumed from stream order.\"\n    70\t  [response call-id]\n    71\t  (when-not (apply2/response? response)\n    72\t    (throw (ex-info \"FFI response envelope is malformed\" {:response response})))\n    73\t  (when (and call-id (not= call-id (apply2/response-id response)))\n    74\t    (throw (ex-info \"FFI response does not correlate with this parked call\"\n    75\t                    {:call-id call-id,\n    76\t                     :response-id (apply2/response-id response)})))\n    77\t  (if-let [err (apply2/response-error response)]\n    78\t    (throw (ex-info (str \"FFI call failed: \"\n    79\t                         (:dao.stream.apply/message err))\n    80\t                    {:call-id call-id, :error err}))\n    81\t    (apply2/response-ok response)))\n    82\t\n    83\t\n    84\t(defn normalize\n    85\t  \"Normalize bridge input to explicit bridge state, without minting.\n    86\t\n    87\t   Accepted forms: nil, a handler map, or an explicit\n    88\t   {:handlers ... :cursor ...} map.\"\n    89\t  [bridge]\n    90\t  (cond (nil? bridge) nil\n    91\t        (and (map? bridge) (contains? bridge :handlers))\n    92\t        (update bridge :handlers #(or % {}))\n    93\t        (map? bridge) {:handlers bridge}\n    94\t        :else (throw (ex-info\n    95\t                       \"Bridge must be nil, a handler map, or {:handlers ... :cursor ...}\"\n    96\t                       {:bridge bridge}))))\n    97\t\n    98\t\n    99\t(defn attach\n   100\t  \"Attach explicit bridge state to a built VM, minting the bridge cursor on\n   101\t   the call-in stream when the bridge does not carry one.\"\n   102\t  [vm bridge]\n   103\t  (if-let [bridge* (normalize bridge)]\n   104\t    (let [{:keys [call-in]} (require-call-pair! (:store vm) :bridge)\n   105\t          cursor (or (:cursor bridge*) (vm/mint-oldest call-in :bridge))]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
